window.SEARCH_INDEX = [["CIV-3.01","PCLL8010","LG3","computing-time","Computing time under O.3","computing time under o.3 the counting engine every other deadline in this lecture runs on — after/from, before, clear days, specified days, and the vacation. an order made on friday 12 september requires a step \"within 7 days after\" it — and 13 september is a saturday, 15 september a general holiday. a summons must be served \"not less than 2 clear days before\" a hearing listed for 4 september; the question is the last safe day to serve. a rule allows 14 days and the period runs 25 july to 8 august, straddling the summer vacation. a defence is due on a day the registry is shut for a black rainstorm warning, and it is filed the next morning. a period of 10 days includes two saturdays and a sunday, and a party assumes those come out of the count. the last step in the action was a summons in march 2024 on which no order was made; the plaintiff now wants to proceed. a party is already three weeks out of time and asks whether an extension can still be granted. both sides' solicitors have agreed in writing to extend the time for serving a pleading, and no summons has been issued. the facts give the date the writ was posted, but the rule counts from the date of service — you need the deemed date before you can count at all. a notice of appeal must be given within one month and the month falls in august, but the case is in the district court."],["CIV-3.02","PCLL8010","LG3","serving-the-writ","Serving the writ under O.10","serving the writ under o.10 personal service as the rule, the two alternatives within the jurisdiction, and the deemed date each produces. the process server hands the envelope to the defendant, who says \"i'm not taking that\" and walks off; the server calls after him that they are court documents and drops them at his feet. a copy of the writ was sent by registered post to the defendant's last known address on 1 october; the plaintiff needs the date service is deemed to have occurred. the writ was pushed under the defendant's front door rather than through the letter box. the writ was posted to a flat the defendant left two years ago, and the plaintiff has a newer address on file but used the old one. the defendant was in singapore on the day the writ went through the letter box, and returned a fortnight later. the defendant telephoned the plaintiff's solicitors about the writ on the third day after posting, and the plaintiff wants to rely on that date instead of the seventh. the registered letter comes back marked \"returned to sender\" and the plaintiff proposes to swear the affidavit of service anyway. the defendant's solicitors write \"we are instructed to accept service\" and indorse the writ, but file no acknowledgement. the writ served on the defendant was a photocopy carrying no seal of the high court, and no form no. 14 was enclosed. a supply contract says process may be served on the defendant at its wan chai office, and the writ was served there. the claim is for possession of a shop unit and the plaintiff proposes to serve by fixing a copy to the shutter."],["CIV-3.03","PCLL8010","LG3","serving-particular-parties","Serving particular kinds of party","serving particular kinds of party companies, overseas companies, partnerships, persons under disability, agents of overseas principals — each has its own route. the defendant is a hong kong company; the writ was posted by ordinary post to its registered office on a monday, and the question is the deemed date. a process server leaves the writ with the receptionist on the ground floor of the tower, though the registered office is on the 14th floor and he was not refused access. the plaintiff's solicitors email a pdf of the writ to the company's general enquiries address and treat that as service on the registered office. the defendant company was struck off and has no registered office, so the statutory route is unavailable. the defendant is a non-hong kong company whose authorised representative is shown in the companies registry but refuses to accept service. an overseas company closed its hong kong office eight months ago and shows no registered office here. the defendant is a firm sued in its own name; the writ is left with the manager at its principal place of business, with no notice saying in what capacity he is served. the plaintiff wants to enforce the eventual judgment against one partner's own flat, and is deciding whom to serve. the partnership was dissolved before the writ was issued and the plaintiff knew it. the defendant is 16 years old, and the plaintiff proposes to serve the writ on him personally at school. a hong kong agent negotiated and signed the contract here for a principal in jakarta, and still acts for that principal. the writ is left with the chairman of the defendant company rather than sent to its registered office."],["CIV-3.04","PCLL8010","LG3","substituted-service","Substituted service","substituted service when the prescribed methods are impracticable — the test, the ex parte application, and what the court needs to see. six attempts at personal service at three known addresses have failed, and a neighbour says the defendant moved out months ago. the defendant is plainly evading the process server: the door is answered, the name confirmed, then the door is shut. the only live channel to the defendant is a facebook messenger account she uses daily. the defendant is known only by a cryptocurrency wallet address and an online handle. the defendant company has no registered office and no place of business anyone can identify. the plaintiff proposes newspaper advertisements as well as email, and the court asks what the advertisements add. the limitation period expires in three weeks and the writ is still unserved. an order permitted service by email to a named address; the plaintiff sent it to a different address at the same domain. judgment was entered in default after substituted service by advertisement, and the defendant says she never saw it. the defendant is in a jurisdiction whose law forbids service of foreign process by a private person."],["CIV-3.05","PCLL8010","LG3","ordinary-service-and-timing","Ordinary service, timing and proof","ordinary service, timing and proof everything that is not an originating process — how it is served, when it counts, what the affidavit must say. a summons is left at the other side's solicitors' office at 4.45 p.m. on a thursday, and the time for responding runs from service. a notice is left at a document exchange box on tuesday; the question is the day it counts as served. an affidavit is served on a saturday, which is also the day a black rainstorm warning is in force. a pleading is posted to a party who gave no address for service and is not represented. the defendant gave an address for service that turns out to be an empty office with no connection to him. a party has never acknowledged service, and the plaintiff asks whether interlocutory documents have to go to him at all. a document is sent as a pdf attachment and the recipient says email is not a method of service. the affidavit of service says only that the document \"was duly served on the defendant\" and gives the date. a writ is to be served on a sunday because the defendant is leaving hong kong on the monday. the other side changed solicitors last month and filed a notice of change; documents went to the old firm."],["CIV-3.06","PCLL8010","LG3","defective-service","Defective service and the Court's discretion","defective service and the court's discretion what happens when service does not comply — o.2 r.1, the cases, and the two sides' options. after months of correspondence between the two firms, the writ is delivered to the defendant's office and left with the receptionist instead of served personally. the writ is served on the general counsel of a firm of accountants rather than on a partner, with a long history of prior dealings between the parties. the writ was served in sydney before any leave to serve out had been obtained, and the plaintiff now applies to put it right. the writ's validity expired on 2 june and it was served on 9 june, no extension having been obtained. the company was served by leaving the writ at the ground-floor reception when the registered office was upstairs — the statutory method was not used at all. the defendant filed an acknowledgement of service and then said the writ was never properly served on him. the defendant wrote to the plaintiff about the claim a week after the irregular service, and instructed solicitors. the writ served carried no seal and no form no. 14, and both sides are represented by experienced solicitors. the defendant acknowledged service, let the time for serving a defence go by, and only then complained about the manner of service. the plaintiff asks the court to declare that what was done stands as good service, and the defendant points to no prejudice of any kind."],["CIV-3.07","PCLL8010","LG3","service-out-of-jurisdiction","Service out of the jurisdiction (O.11)","service out of the jurisdiction (o.11) is a gateway in o.11 r.1 open, will the court give leave, and by what method may the writ lawfully be served abroad? the defendant is a shenzhen company with no place of business in hong kong, and the writ has to reach it. a distribution agreement was signed in hong kong and is governed by hong kong law; the distributor is in bangkok. a contract was made in taipei but the failure to deliver occurred in hong kong. a defective component shipped from osaka caused a factory fire in tsuen wan. a hong kong defendant has been duly served, and the plaintiff wants to join a related company incorporated in the bvi. service must be effected in macao, and the plaintiff proposes to instruct local agents to hand the writ over. the plaintiff lodges a request for service in the mainland with two copies of the writ and no translation. the translation of the writ is certified correct by a translator who gives a name and address but no qualifications. leave was granted on an ex parte application at which the plaintiff did not mention a parallel action abroad. the order giving leave says nothing about the time for acknowledging service, and the defendant assumes 14 days. the destination is a hague convention country and the plaintiff must pick a channel. the claim is for an interim injunction in aid of foreign proceedings, and the question is whether a gateway is open at all."],["CIV-3.08","PCLL8010","LG3","acknowledgement-of-service","Acknowledgement of service (O.12)","acknowledgement of service (o.12) who may acknowledge, on what form, to what address, by when — and what filing it does and does not commit the defendant to. a writ was served personally on monday 6 october; the defendant asks when the acknowledgement is due and whether the day of service counts. the writ was posted on 1 october, so the deemed date of service is the seventh day after — and the acknowledgement period runs from that date, not from posting. the defendant ticks the box on form no. 14 saying he does not intend to contest the proceedings. the defendant wants to dispute the court's jurisdiction and asks whether acknowledging service gives that away. two defendants were served on different dates and only one has acknowledged. the acknowledgement was filed a day late, and the plaintiff has not yet entered judgment. the acknowledgement was filed late, but only after the plaintiff had entered judgment in default that morning. the writ was served out of the jurisdiction under an order that limited the time for acknowledgement to 28 days. the defendant is a company and the acknowledgement is signed by a director rather than by solicitors. the defendant filed no acknowledgement but wrote a letter to the court saying he denies owing anything."],["CIV-3.09","PCLL8010","LG3","contesting-jurisdiction","Contesting jurisdiction and forum non conveniens (O.12 r.8)","contesting jurisdiction and forum non conveniens (o.12 r.8) the two limbs of o.12 r.8, the procedure that preserves the objection, the spiliada test, jurisdiction clauses and a stay for arbitration. the defendant was served abroad under an order giving leave, and says the gateway relied on was never open on these facts. the defendant says the writ was not duly served on him and wants a declaration to that effect. the contract contains an exclusive jurisdiction clause in favour of the singapore courts. parallel proceedings between the same parties are already running in the mainland. the defendant acknowledged service, then served a defence on the merits, and now wants to challenge jurisdiction. the time limited for serving a defence has passed and no o.12 r.8 summons has been issued. the defendant applies to set aside service and, in the alternative, for a stay on forum grounds. every witness and document is in jakarta, but the contract is governed by hong kong law. the defendant applied in time but the summons asks only for a stay, not for the service to be set aside. the plaintiff says the defendant submitted to the jurisdiction by taking part in an interlocutory application."],["CIV-4.01","PCLL8010","LG4","what-pleadings-do","What pleadings are and why they matter","what pleadings are and why they matter the types of pleading, the function the cases give them, and the consequence — the parties are bound by what they plead. at trial the plaintiff wants to run a misrepresentation case that appears nowhere in the statement of claim. the defendant resists a discovery request for three years of emails, saying it ranges well beyond the pleaded issues. the defence says only that the goods \"were not as described\", and the plaintiff writes that it cannot tell what case it has to meet. counsel proposes to serve a rejoinder after the reply, without applying to the court. the defendant says it was ambushed at trial by an allegation of dishonesty first raised in opening. the action was begun by originating summons and the defendant asks when its defence is due. a statement of claim is plainly bad in law, and the defendant is choosing between a request for particulars, a strike-out and letting it go to trial. you are asked for the purpose pleadings serve, and the answer wants poon hau kei and aktieselskabet dansk rather than a recital of o.18."],["CIV-4.02","PCLL8010","LG4","pleadings-timetable","The pleadings timetable and close of pleadings","the pleadings timetable and close of pleadings who serves what, by when, in each of the two writ scenarios — and the date that decides whether an amendment needs leave. a generally indorsed writ was served on 6 october and the defendant gave notice of intention to defend on 14 october; no statement of claim has followed. a specially indorsed writ was served on 1 september; the defendant asks when the defence is due, and both the acknowledgement deadline and the date of the statement of claim have to be computed. the statement of claim was served three weeks after the writ, so the later of the two reference dates is not the acknowledgement deadline. the plaintiff served an o.14 summons before the defence fell due, and leave to defend was given on 20 november. the defendant issued an o.12 r.8 summons disputing jurisdiction, and it was dismissed on 3 march. a defence was served on 10 april; no reply and no defence to counterclaim followed, and a party wants to amend on 5 may. a reply was served on 2 june and the plaintiff assumes it can still amend without leave on 20 june. a request for further and better particulars is outstanding, and the defendant says the pleadings cannot have closed. a defence was served on the plaintiff but never filed in the registry, and the time for filing has gone. counsel wants to serve an amended statement of claim on 12 august, with no leave and no consent. the defence counts its 28 days by excluding the saturdays and sundays in the period."],["CIV-4.03","PCLL8010","LG4","formal-requirements","Formal requirements for a pleading","formal requirements for a pleading what must appear on the face of every pleading, how it is divided, how it is indorsed and signed — and the amendment colours. the draft defence is headed only \"in the high court\" with no action number and no year of issue. a pleading reads \"the defendant agreed to buy two hundred cartons for one hundred and fifty thousand dollars on the first of september\". one paragraph of the statement of claim alleges the contract, the breach, the loss and a separate misrepresentation together. the pleading is served by an agency firm in hong kong on the instructions of a firm in kowloon, and carries only the agent's name. the plaintiff acts in person and the pleading is indorsed with the name of the solicitors he consulted once. counsel settled the pleading and his name is printed, not signed, at the end. the pleading carries a statement of truth signed by the client but no signature under o.18 r.6(5). a re-amended statement of claim is marked up in red, the same colour as the first amendment. an amended defence carries no indorsement saying when it was amended or under what order or rule. the amendments run to forty paragraphs and the marked-up document has become unreadable."],["CIV-4.04","PCLL8010","LG4","statement-of-truth","The statement of truth (O.41A)","the statement of truth (o.41a) which documents need one, who may sign, in what form, and what happens if it is missing or false. the party is a company and the statement of truth is signed by a sales manager who gives no office or position. the plaintiff's solicitor proposes to sign the statement of truth, and signs \"for and on behalf of chan & co\". a defence is served with no statement of truth at all, and the plaintiff wants it struck out rather than corrected. the defendant is a firm, and a consultant with no partnership interest but day-to-day control of the business signs. further and better particulars are served under an o.18 r.12(3) order, unverified. an amended statement of claim is served and only the original was verified. the plaintiff sues by a next friend, and the statement of truth is in the plaintiff's own name. the statement of truth is a separate document that names the parties but does not say which pleading it verifies or when that pleading was served. the defence pleads two inconsistent alternative accounts and the solicitor says it cannot honestly be verified. an allegation verified by the client turns out to have been known by him to be false, and the other side wants contempt proceedings. the insurer conducting the defence wants to sign rather than the insured. a witness statement is served without a statement of truth, and the party assumes the consequence is the same as for a pleading."],["CIV-4.05","PCLL8010","LG4","material-facts-not-evidence","Material facts, not evidence — and points of law","material facts, not evidence — and points of law the o.18 r.7 line between what goes in and what stays out, and the difference between raising a point of law and arguing one. the statement of claim pleads that \"the plaintiff sent a purchase order dated 3 may to the defendant\" rather than that the parties agreed to buy and sell. a paragraph pleads that the hotel's cctv recording shows the minibus striking the bmw. the pleading sets out a warranty clause word for word over half a page. a paragraph reads: \"for a contract to be formed there must be offer and acceptance and an intention to create legal relations; all were present here.\" a paragraph begins \"the plaintiff submits that the defendant's conduct as pleaded above constitutes acceptance\". the pleading says only \"the defendant was negligent\" and the defendant says that is a bare legal conclusion. the claim is in defamation and the plaintiff wants to set out the words complained of. the seller delivered the goods to a carrier, and the plaintiff wonders whether delivery to the buyer has to be pleaded. the defendant has not pleaded mitigation and the plaintiff assumes it must be pleaded by whoever raises it. the goods could not have been delivered unless the deposit had been paid, and the deposit is not pleaded. the statement of claim never names the cause of action anywhere, and the defence says the claim is bad for that reason alone. the pleading runs to sixty paragraphs of narrative history, and the defendant complains of length rather than content."],["CIV-4.06","PCLL8010","LG4","pleading-specifically-and-particulars","Matters to be pleaded specifically, and particulars","matters to be pleaded specifically, and particulars what o.18 r.8 forces into a later pleading, what o.18 r.12(1) requires to be particularised, and the criminal-conviction rule. the defence wants to rely on the limitation period but pleads only a bare denial of liability. the defendant says the plaintiff walked into the road without looking, and the defence does not use the words contributory negligence. the defendant has a cross-claim for unpaid fees and wants it to reduce the plaintiff's claim, but the defence never mentions set-off. the defence says the plaintiff could have re-let the premises within a month, pleaded nowhere in terms. the statement of claim claims exemplary damages in the prayer with no facts pleaded to support them. the plaintiff claims interest but the pleading cites no statutory or contractual basis for it. the claim alleges fraud in a single sentence with no particulars of the representations or who made them. the pleading alleges the defendant acted with malice and gives no facts from which malice is to be inferred. the pleading alleges the defendant knew of the influencer collaboration, and the question is whether the facts behind that knowledge must be set out. the particulars of loss run to about 400 words of itemised expenses set out in the body of the pleading. the claim is for personal injuries and the statement of claim is served with no medical report. the defendants pleaded guilty in the magistracy to permitting a vehicle with defective tyres to be driven, and the plaintiff wants to rely on the convictions. the defence is to an action for recovery of land and pleads only that the defendant's tenant is in possession."],["CIV-4.07","PCLL8010","LG4","further-and-better-particulars","Further and better particulars","further and better particulars the letter first, then the application — the test, the burden, and what happens if an order is not complied with. the statement of claim alleges \"various breaches of the agreement\" without identifying a single term. the defendant issues a summons for particulars having never written to ask for them first. a request was made by letter, refused in terms, and the applicant now issues a summons. the particulars sought are plainly relevant, but the applicant cannot say the case cannot be tried fairly without them. the plaintiff asks for particulars of the defence before any defence has been served. the statement of claim is so vague that the defendant genuinely cannot draft a defence to it. the particulars served set out answers in a numbered list without reproducing the requests they answer. particulars are served in response to an order and carry no statement of truth. an order for particulars has been ignored twice, and the applicant wants an unless order. the party finally offers the particulars at trial, having never served them, and wants to call evidence on the point. a master refused the order and the applicant appeals, saying the particulars were obviously relevant."],["CIV-4.08","PCLL8010","LG4","pleading-in-the-alternative","Inconsistent allegations and pleading in the alternative","inconsistent allegations and pleading in the alternative o.18 r.10 and o.18 r.12a do different jobs — one governs a previous pleading, the other the same pleading. the defence says the defendant never signed the contract; the same defence then says that if he did sign it, it contained terms the plaintiff has not pleaded. the defence says the defendant had no agreement with the plaintiff on the written contract; in the alternative it admits an agreement made orally on different terms. the reply gives a materially different account of the same meeting from the one in the statement of claim, though both versions could in theory be true. the defendant wants to raise a new ground of claim in the reply that contradicts his own defence. a party's later pleading contradicts his earlier one, and he says o.18 r.10(2) entitles him to leave it there. the party wants to put both versions into the earlier pleading instead, and the pleadings closed a month ago. the alternatives are pleaded and the solicitor asks whether a statement of truth can be given for both. both alternatives answer the same question — did the defendant sign? — and the second gives the first away. the second alternative is a complete answer on a different factual footing from the first."],["CIV-4.09","PCLL8010","LG4","statement-of-claim","Drafting the statement of claim","drafting the statement of claim the prayer for relief, the cause-of-action constraint tied to the writ, and the date the writ was issued. the client wants an injunction as well as damages, and the prayer asks only for damages and costs. the prayer omits costs, and the plaintiff's solicitor asks whether that is fatal. the writ was generally indorsed for breach of contract, and the statement of claim adds a claim in negligent misstatement arising from the same negotiations. the statement of claim pleads a wholly separate conversion claim about different goods from a different year. the plaintiff wants to enlarge the damages figure from the one indorsed on the writ, without amending the writ. the statement of claim bears the action number and the year of issue, but nowhere states the date the writ was issued. the claim is for personal injuries and neither a medical report nor a statement of special damages accompanies the statement of claim. the statement of claim specifically pleads limitation, and the drafter assumes o.18 r.8 applies to it. the judge is asked at trial to grant a declaration that was never claimed. the statement of claim runs to nine pages of background and the defendant says it is anything but brief."],["CIV-4.10","PCLL8010","LG4","defence-counterclaim-and-reply","Defence, counterclaim and reply","defence, counterclaim and reply the traverse rules that decide what is admitted, set-off, the counterclaim as a separate action, and joinder of issue. the defence opens: \"the defendant denies each and every allegation in the statement of claim as if the same were set out and traversed seriatim.\" the defence denies the allegation of late delivery but never says what the defendant says actually happened. the defence says nothing at all about paragraph 7, but sets out a full positive case on the same issue elsewhere. the defendant genuinely has no idea whether the plaintiff paid the third party, and wants to put the plaintiff to proof. the defendant knows for a fact the meeting never took place, and the defence says he \"does not admit\" it. the defendant has an unliquidated cross-claim for defective work and wants it both to reduce the claim and to stand as a claim of its own. the defendant pleads tender before action and has paid nothing into court. the defence and counterclaim are served as two separate documents. the counterclaim is against the plaintiff and a company that is not yet a party and is said to be liable along with the plaintiff. the plaintiff obtains judgment on the claim and assumes the counterclaim falls away with it. the plaintiff serves no reply to the defence, and the defendant says the defence therefore stands admitted. the defendant purports to answer the statement of claim by joining issue on it."],["CIV-4.11","PCLL8010","LG4","amending-pleadings","Amending pleadings (O.20)","amending pleadings (o.20) three routes — without leave, by agreement, with leave — and the limitation-period exceptions. the plaintiff wants to amend the statement of claim for the first time, and the pleadings closed a fortnight ago. the plaintiff amends the statement of claim without leave before the close of pleadings, and the defendant wants it disallowed. an amended pleading was served 20 days ago and the other side only now objects. both sides agree in writing to the amendment, but it adds a second defendant. the amendment is sought on the first day of trial and the other side says its witnesses are no longer available. the writ names \"wing hing trading limited\" where the correct defendant is \"wing hing trading company limited\", and limitation expired last month. the plaintiff obtained a grant of probate after issuing the writ and now wants to sue as executor. a new cause of action is proposed after limitation has run, on facts that overlap substantially but not completely with the pleaded claim. leave to amend was granted 20 days ago, the order specified no period, and nothing has been filed. the amendment is opposed as a late change of case, and the applicant offers to pay the costs thrown away. the court raises of its own motion that a pleading should be amended, and the other side says it is not necessary for a fair disposal. the amended pleading is served in red without any indorsement of the order that authorised it."],["CIV-4.12","PCLL8010","LG4","striking-out","Striking out a pleading (O.18 r.19)","striking out a pleading (o.18 r.19) the four grounds, the evidence rule that splits them, and the plain-and-obvious threshold. the statement of claim pleads a cause of action that does not exist in hong kong law, on any view of the facts. the defendant applies under ground (a) and files a 20-page affidavit exhibiting the correspondence. the pleading accuses the other side's director of taking bribes, on an issue that has nothing to do with the claim. the plaintiff has sued the same defendant on the same facts twice before and lost both times. the pleading is rambling and ambiguous, and the defendant says it cannot fairly be tried in that form. the claim has a slim chance of success but turns on disputed facts about what was said at a meeting. the defect in the pleading could be cured by amendment, and the applicant asks for it to be struck out anyway. the applicant issues the summons three days before the hearing without writing to the other side first. the defence carries no statement of truth, and the plaintiff wants it struck out rather than ordering verification. the proceedings were begun by originating summons, and the respondent says o.18 r.19 has no application. the plaintiff has no intention of ever bringing the action to trial and is using it to pressure the defendant."],["CIV-5.01","PCLL8010","LG5","function-and-structure","What a statement of claim must do, and its four-part shape","what a statement of claim must do, and its four-part shape the function the lecture gives it in one sentence, and the skeleton every draft is hung on. you are handed a page of client instructions and asked to draft the statement of claim from scratch. a draft pleads every legal ingredient but never says what the plaintiff wants the court to do. a draft opens with three paragraphs about the parties' twenty-year trading relationship in a claim on a single invoice. a paragraph in the draft supports no ingredient of the cause of action and no head of relief. the draft recites what the client's bookkeeper will say in the witness box. the facts disclose nothing of significance about the parties, and the drafter is unsure whether the introduction can be dropped. the defendant's knowledge was acquired in the same telephone call in which the terms were agreed, and the drafter wonders whether the appendix order can be departed from. before a word of the draft is written, you have to be able to say in one sentence what the document is for."],["CIV-5.02","PCLL8010","LG5","case-analysis-tools","Dramatis Personae and the chronology","dramatis personae and the chronology the two organising tools that turn a bundle of instructions into facts you can plead — and a list of what is still missing. the instructions run to eight pages of correspondence and attendance notes, in no particular order. three companies and five individuals appear in the papers and it is not yet clear who should be a defendant. the client says the deposit was paid \"around october 2020 or before\" and cannot be more precise. a key meeting is described in the instructions with no document, no note and no witness named. the client refers throughout to \"the manager\" and it is unclear which company employed him. you need to settle the defined terms — \"the agreement\", \"the collision\" — before writing a word of the draft. someone in the papers merely watched the accident happen, and the question is whether they are a party or a witness. you have to write the letter asking the client for further instructions, and need a list of what is still missing."],["CIV-5.03","PCLL8010","LG5","five-level-case-analysis","The 5-Level Case Analysis","the 5-level case analysis area of law, cause of action, ingredients, material facts, evidence — and the four separate jobs the one grid does. the same facts might found both a contractual claim and a claim in negligence, and the drafter is unsure whether that is one grid or two. the draft pleads the contract, the terms and the breach, but nothing about what the defendant was told before contracting. there is no material fact anywhere in the papers against one of the ingredients of the cause of action. a material fact is pleaded and nobody has yet asked how it would be proved at trial. the client wants to know both whether the claim is worth running and what the pleading will say. there are two defendants, and the drafter proposes one set of particulars of negligence covering both. the employer is sued as well as the driver, and vicarious liability has not been treated as an ingredient of its own. the client's account of the loss is plausible but there is no invoice, no receipt and no bank record for it. a statutory breach is relied on and there has been a conviction, with a date the papers do not give."],["CIV-5.04","PCLL8010","LG5","drafting-process","The nine-step drafting process","the nine-step drafting process part c’s steps 1 to 9 — from gathering the facts to explaining the statement of truth to the client. the client has sent the papers and asks for a draft by friday; nothing has been organised yet. you are asked to summarise the claim in two or three sentences before drafting anything. the plan needs to record that the express delivery terms are pleaded only because performance will be pleaded by reference to them. the instructions say the deposit was $8,000; a later email says $8,900. the vehicle registration number is nowhere in the papers and the draft needs it. the draft is finished and nobody has yet read it through as the opposing solicitor would. the draft is ready to go to the client and the statement of truth has not been explained to him. the client will sign the statement of truth without being told what a false statement exposes him to. a paragraph in the draft cannot be placed in any of the o.18 categories the drafter is working to."],["CIV-5.05","PCLL8010","LG5","contract-claim-structure","Basic structure for a contractual claim","basic structure for a contractual claim appendix 6’s eleven steps — parties, contract, terms, d’s knowledge, performance, breach, consequences, loss, interest, prayer, verification. the agreement was made by telephone between a director of each company and there is no written contract. the contract was partly in writing and partly oral, and the drafter is unsure how much of the conversation to set out. the original agreement was varied by a later exchange, and the variation is not in dispute. both companies contracted through agents, and the draft never pleads their authority to act. the plaintiff relies on terms implied by the sale of goods ordinance and gives no basis for the implication. the plaintiff wants to treat the contract as at an end, and the draft alleges breach but neither repudiation nor acceptance. the buyer told the seller before contracting that the machines were for a wedding banquet on a fixed date, and the draft omits it. the draft claims loss of regular profits with nothing pleaded about what the defendant knew at the time of the contract. the draft says the plaintiff \"will refer to all other terms and conditions of the agreement at trial\". the claim is for a fixed sum due under a guarantee and the draft calls it \"loss and damage\". one head of loss cannot be quantified until after discovery. the plaintiff spent money trying to find a replacement supplier and wants those costs back. the action is in the district court and the draft ends at the prayer for relief."],["CIV-5.06","PCLL8010","LG5","tort-claim-structure","Basic structure for a tortious claim","basic structure for a tortious claim appendix 7’s nine steps, the neutral-accident rule at step 2, separate particulars per defendant, and o.18 r.7a where there is a conviction. a bus mounted the pavement, struck a parked vehicle and pushed it through a shopfront; you act for the shop owner. the accident paragraph in the draft already alleges that the driver was careless. it is a straightforward traffic accident and the drafter has written two paragraphs on the existence of a duty of care. the claim is in passing off and the draft pleads the misrepresentation before identifying any goodwill. the driver was on his usual shift, and the draft never pleads that he was acting in the course of his employment. both the driver and the bus company are sued, and the particulars of negligence are run together in one list. the driver pleaded guilty in the magistracy and the plaintiff wants to rely on the conviction at trial. the plaintiff was injured and the draft attaches no medical report and files no statement of special damages. the stock was destroyed, the ceiling damaged and the shop closed for six weeks, and the business lost is not yet quantified. the plaintiff replaced a ten-year-old vehicle with a new one and the defendant will say that is betterment. the events after the breach have already been described in the accident paragraph. the plaintiff claims contractual interest in a negligence action."],["CIV-5.07","PCLL8010","LG5","interest-and-prayer","Interest, the prayer for relief and the District Court plea","interest, the prayer for relief and the district court plea where interest goes and where it must not, what the prayer has to contain, and the extra paragraph a district court action needs. the draft claims interest in the prayer only, and the interest claimed is contractual. the draft claims discretionary interest in the prayer alone, and the drafter is told that is wrong. the claim is for a liquidated sum and the prayer asks for \"damages\". the client wants a declaration and an injunction, and the prayer asks only for damages, interest and costs. the prayer ends with \"further or other relief\" and nothing else is specifically claimed. the prayer omits costs entirely. the action is in the district court and there is no paragraph pleading that the relief falls within its jurisdiction. the jurisdiction plea is there but cites no section of the district court ordinance. the action is in the court of first instance and the drafter has added a jurisdiction plea anyway. the statement of truth is signed by a company's sales manager, who does not state his position."],["CIV-5.08","PCLL8010","LG5","drafting-guide-criteria","The Pleadings Drafting Guide — what the draft is marked against","the pleadings drafting guide — what the draft is marked against appendix 8: presentation 30%, contents 40%, overall 30% — and how to run it over a draft as a checklist. the draft is finished and you are asked to mark it, or to say how it would be marked. the pleading is legally complete but the paragraphs are unnumbered and the headings inconsistent. paragraph 4 says the deposit was paid and paragraph 9 says it was never paid. every ingredient of the cause of action has a material fact, but the particulars required by o.18 r.12 are missing. the draft is neat, well-organised and pleads a claim that cannot succeed. the draft contains a paragraph explaining what negligence is. marks are split three ways — presentation, contents, overall — and the draft has to earn each block separately. you are about to start drafting and have not read the criteria the draft will be assessed against."],["CIV-5.09","PCLL8010","LG5","worked-contract-claim","Worked example: the commercial dishwashers claim","worked example: the commercial dishwashers claim exercise 1 end to end — the analysis, the summary, the skeletal plan and what the draft extract does with them. a restaurant bought two commercial dishwashers by telephone; both had defective temperature control modules and one a faulty water pump. the buyer told the seller in the same call that the machines were for a celebrity wedding banquet and that poor hygiene damages a restaurant's reputation. the banquet guests fell ill, the restaurant gave the customer a discount, paid for repairs and lost regular custom. you need to see how a pre-contract conversation is pleaded ahead of the express terms, and why. you need a worked \"particulars of breach\" and \"particulars of damages\" to model your own on. the deposit may have been 10% of the dishwashers alone or of a larger order, and the plan has to handle the uncertainty. the action is in the district court and you need to see where interest under s49 of the district court ordinance goes. you want to see a statement of truth signed by a company director, stating his position and his authority."],["CIV-5.10","PCLL8010","LG5","worked-tort-claim","Worked example: the shopfront collision claim","worked example: the shopfront collision claim exercise 2 end to end — two defendants, vicarious liability, particulars per defendant, and property loss that is not all quantified. a bus driver fell asleep at the wheel after working hours well beyond the transport department guidelines, and the bus's steering rod was severely rusted. the shop owner sues both the driver and the bus company, and you need to see how the two sets of particulars are laid out. you need a model of a genuinely neutral accident paragraph that a defendant might admit. you want to see where vicarious liability is pleaded, and where the facts founding it go. step 3 on duty of care is not applicable and you need to see a plan that says so and moves on. the registration numbers are not yet known and you need to see how a draft carries a visible placeholder. two heads of loss are unquantified and one raises betterment, and you want to see them left open rather than guessed. the action is in the court of first instance and interest is claimed under s48 of the high court ordinance."],["CIV-6.01","PCLL8010","LG6","liquidated-or-unliquidated","Classifying the claim: liquidated or unliquidated","classifying the claim: liquidated or unliquidated the first question in the lecture — it fixes which rule applies, which form is served, and whether the judgment is final or interlocutory. the writ is indorsed for $380,000 due under an invoice, and the plaintiff wants judgment entered for that figure without an assessment. the writ claims $1,000,000 for loss of goodwill — a precise figure, but one the court would have to assess. the writ claims $200,000 lent plus a further $20,000 agreed as interest on the loan. the writ claims a debt of $90,000 plus interest under s48 at 2% above the judgment-debt rate. the writ claims unpaid rent of $60,000 and damages for the state in which the premises were left. the defendant wants to admit that same mixed claim under o.13a, and assumes the liquidated half stays liquidated. the writ claims delivery up of a container of goods and nothing else. the writ claims possession of a flat and mesne profits. the writ claims only a declaration and an injunction, and the defendant has never acknowledged service. the plaintiff says the sum is \"obviously calculable\" from documents the defendant holds."],["CIV-6.02","PCLL8010","LG6","admissions-o13a","Making an admission under O.13A","making an admission under o.13a when a defendant who does not want to fight can admit, by when, on which form, and what the admission actually commits him to. the writ claims $150,000 and delivery up of the plant; the defendant wants to admit and pay. the defendant admits the whole of a $150,000 debt and wants to know which form to file. the defendant admits owing $90,000 of a $150,000 claim and offers it in satisfaction of everything. the defendant accepts he caused the accident but says the repair figure is far too high. the defendant accepts liability for the accident and offers $40,000 in satisfaction of the whole claim. the time for serving the defence expired last week and no default judgment has yet been entered. the plaintiff entered judgment under o.13 yesterday morning and the admission arrives that afternoon. the defendant filed the admission in the registry but has not yet served a copy on the plaintiff. the defendant is a company and files form no. 16 without its latest audited accounts. the defendant ticked the acknowledgement of service to say he does not intend to defend, and now wants to admit under o.13a. the writ was served without any form no. 16 or form no. 16c, and the plaintiff now applies for default judgment. having admitted liability under r.6, the defendant is surprised to learn his exposure is not capped. the defendant admitted three weeks ago and now wants to withdraw the admission."],["CIV-6.03","PCLL8010","LG6","responding-to-an-admission","The plaintiff's response to an admission","the plaintiff's response to an admission four rules, four forms and one 14-day clock — and in every case the sanction for missing it is a stay of the claim. a copy of a full admission of a $150,000 liquidated claim was served on 4 march; the plaintiff files nothing by 18 march. the defendant admits $90,000 of a $150,000 claim in satisfaction of the whole, and the plaintiff wants the balance as well. the plaintiff wants to take the $90,000 now and carry on for the rest. the plaintiff refuses the part admission on a liquidated claim, and asks what happens to the action. the defendant has admitted liability on an unliquidated claim and offered nothing; the plaintiff wants judgment. the defendant offered $40,000 on an unliquidated claim and the plaintiff thinks it is worth $120,000. the plaintiff filed the request but wants to specify payment by monthly instalments. the plaintiff files the request and expects a hearing before judgment is entered. the claim was stayed for missing the 14 days, and the plaintiff asks whether the action is dead. the indorsement never stated the basis of the claim to interest, and the request for judgment includes an interest calculation. judgment is being entered under r.6 and the plaintiff wants the interest included in it."],["CIV-6.04","PCLL8010","LG6","time-to-pay","Requesting time to pay, instalments and the stay of execution","requesting time to pay, instalments and the stay of execution how a defendant who admits but cannot pay gets terms — who decides the rate, what the stay of execution is worth, and how easily it is lost. the defendant admits the whole claim but says he can pay only $3,000 a month out of his salary. the defendant files the admission now and proposes to apply for time to pay next week. the defendant admits liability on an unliquidated claim under r.6 and asks for instalments. the plaintiff accepts the defendant's proposal of twelve monthly instalments. the plaintiff rejects the instalment proposal, and the defendant fears he loses the benefit of admitting. the court is to fix the rate without a hearing, and the defendant wants to be heard. the rate was determined without a hearing on 1 may and the defendant wants it looked at again. the defendant has paid four instalments in full and the fifth is $200 short. judgment was entered on instalments determined by the court after the plaintiff objected, and the defendant assumes execution is stayed. the defendant weighs a fixed costs figure of $10,000 and a stay of execution against defending a claim he cannot win. the defendant is an individual acting in person and the plaintiff is legally represented."],["CIV-6.05","PCLL8010","LG6","default-notice-of-intention","Default judgment for failure to give notice of intention to defend (O.13)","default judgment for failure to give notice of intention to defend (o.13) the as-of-right judgment when no acknowledgement of service arrives — when the clock actually runs out, and what it does to the co-defendants. the writ was served personally on 6 october; nothing has been filed and it is now 22 october. the defendant returned an acknowledgement on day 5 saying he does not intend to contest, and the plaintiff wants to move at once. the defendant acknowledged service in time but has served no defence. the writ claims a liquidated sum against d1 and unliquidated damages against d2; only d1 is in default. the writ claims possession of land against three defendants and only one has failed to acknowledge. the writ claims a declaration as to the ownership of shares, and the defendant has never acknowledged. the defendant paid the claim in full after the writ was served, and the plaintiff wants his costs. judgment was applied for after the 14 days but sealed only after the defendant had filed his acknowledgement. the defendant filed his acknowledgement four days late, before any judgment was entered. the defendant wants to acknowledge service now, and judgment was entered last month. the last step in the action was in march last year and the plaintiff now wants to sign judgment. the defendant does not intend to contest but wants execution by fieri facias held off while he applies for a stay."],["CIV-6.06","PCLL8010","LG6","default-of-defence","Default judgment in default of defence (O.19)","default judgment in default of defence (o.19) the second-stage default — and the notice of intention to enter judgment that o.13 does not require and most answers get wrong. the defendant acknowledged service on 10 march and the 28 days for the defence expired on 21 april with nothing served. the plaintiff enters judgment in default of defence without writing to the defendant's solicitors first. written notice of intention to enter judgment was served on monday and judgment entered on wednesday. the notice of intention to enter judgment was served in good time but no affidavit of that service was filed. the defendant never acknowledged service, and the claim is for relief outside o.13 rr.1-5. the defendant counterclaimed and the plaintiff has served no defence to counterclaim. the plaintiff was required to serve a statement of claim and never did, and the defendant wants the action dismissed. the claim is for an injunction and the defendant has served no defence. a severable claim is made against two defendants and only one has failed to serve a defence. the claim is for possession against two defendants; judgment is entered against one and the plaintiff wants to enforce. the defendant argues no notice was needed because he never gave notice of intention to defend — and he did file an acknowledgement."],["CIV-6.07","PCLL8010","LG6","entering-default-judgment","Entering judgment under O.13: procedure, proof of service and fixed costs","entering judgment under o.13: procedure, proof of service and fixed costs the documents that actually obtain an o.13 judgment — proof of service of the writ, which is what o.13 needs because the defendant never acknowledged. o.19 asks a different question (see \"default judgment in default of defence (o.19)\"); only the fixed-costs scale and the form of judgment are genuinely shared between the two. the defendant never acknowledged service and the plaintiff has to prove the writ was duly served. the writ was posted to the defendant's last known address and the affidavit says nothing about when it would have come to his knowledge. the deponent swears the writ would have reached the defendant within 7 days, knowing the defendant moved abroad last year. the affirmation of service says only that the letter was not returned undelivered, and the writ came back before judgment was obtained. the affidavit of service says nothing about the o.13a admission forms having gone out with the writ. the posted writ is returned undelivered a week after judgment was entered, and the plaintiff wants to start execution. the plaintiff wants to know whether the set-aside on a returned writ needs an application or just an affidavit. judgment is entered for a liquidated demand and the plaintiff uses the form for unliquidated damages. the judgment as drawn does not say how or where the writ was served. judgment on a liquidated demand is obtained 40 days after service of the writ, and the plaintiff expects fixed costs. substituted service was ordered and effected on two defendants, and the plaintiff is working out the fixed costs. an interlocutory judgment has been entered in the district court and the plaintiff asks what happens next. the plaintiff assumes the same affidavit of service of the writ will do for an o.19 judgment."],["CIV-6.08","PCLL8010","LG6","regular-or-irregular","Is the default judgment regular or irregular?","is the default judgment regular or irregular? the pivot of every set-aside question — service either complied with the rules or it did not, and the whole test changes with the answer. the writ went through the letter box of a flat the defendant had moved out of, and he says he never saw it. the writ was delivered to the defendant's usual address and signed for by a domestic helper; the defendant was in canada for two months. the defendant simply says \"i never received it\" and offers nothing else. the defendant gives an account of being in hospital for six weeks and of mail piling up unopened. judgment was entered on substituted service by newspaper advertisement and the defendant says he does not read that paper. the posted writ was returned undelivered and the plaintiff entered judgment anyway. a company was served by registered post at its registered office under an affirmation framed on o.10 r.1, and the writ came back undelivered. the plaintiff relied on s827 of the companies ordinance in the covering letter and on o.10 r.1 in the affidavit. judgment was sealed the day after the defendant filed his acknowledgement of service. the writ was served in the prescribed manner and the defendant was simply too busy to deal with it. the defendant wants to know who is likely to pay the costs of the set-aside application before he issues it."],["CIV-6.09","PCLL8010","LG6","setting-aside-irregular","Setting aside an irregular judgment","setting aside an irregular judgment set aside as of right, without the merits — but the court can still impose terms, and the costs run the other way. the writ was posted under o.10 r.1(2)(a) and returned undelivered, and the defendant is about to issue a summons. service did not comply with o.10 at all, and the plaintiff says the defence is hopeless anyway. the defendant can show he never knew of the writ, and has no defence worth the name on the merits. the judgment was irregular and the defendant delayed four months before applying. the defendant wants the judgment set aside and his costs of the application. the default was of defence rather than of acknowledgement, and the defendant is looking for the governing rule. the plaintiff asks the court to impose terms as the price of setting aside. the defendant has already taken a fresh step in the action since learning of the judgment. the plaintiff offers to consent to the set-aside if each side bears its own costs."],["CIV-6.10","PCLL8010","LG6","setting-aside-regular","Setting aside a regular judgment","setting aside a regular judgment a discretion, not a right — explain the default, show a defence with a real prospect of success, and expect to pay the costs. service was in order, the defendant missed the deadline because his solicitor's clerk mislaid the file, and he has a real defence. the defendant explains the default fully but his proposed defence is a bare denial. the defendant has a strong defence on the merits and no explanation at all for the delay. judgment was entered eight months ago and the defendant applies only now. the defendant produces a signed release the plaintiff never mentioned. the plaintiff has already executed against the defendant's bank account. the court is minded to set aside on terms that the judgment sum be paid into court. the defence would succeed on part of the claim only. the defendant says the plaintiff's own conduct caused him to believe no judgment would be entered. the defendant asks what he must put in his affidavit, and how it differs from an irregular-judgment application."],["CIV-7.01","PCLL8010","LG7","drafting-summons","Summonses: drafting, service, evidence and hearing","summonses: drafting, service, evidence and hearing draft the correct application, issue and serve it properly, and distinguish a contested hearing, non-attendance and an ex parte order. a defendant wants security for costs and sends the registry a letter asking for an order instead of issuing a summons. a draft summons lists the orders sought but leaves the left-hand margin blank. a solicitor uses the high court heading on an application in an existing district court action. an application for an injunction has been addressed to a master simply because it is interlocutory. the sealed summons asks for one order, but the applicant now wants to add a second form of relief. a summons has been prepared, but has not yet been sealed, and the applicant describes it as already issued. an ordinary summons is served the day before its listed hearing, and no abridgement has been obtained. a summons seeks only more time to file a document and is served the day before the hearing. a summons combines an extension of time with substantive relief, but the applicant relies on the time-summons exception. the summons was adjourned generally and the applicant now wants to put it back in the list. the respondent fails to attend, and the applicant cannot show that the summons was served. the applicant missed the hearing and the summons was dismissed without argument; in another case it was dismissed after full argument."],["CIV-7.02","PCLL8010","LG7","consent-order-or-summons","Consent order or consent summons?","consent order or consent summons? agreement on the result is only the beginning: check whether o.42 r.5a permits the paper procedure and whether every party has actually agreed. all represented parties agree that a default judgment should be set aside and costs paid. the parties agree an injunction and send an o.42 r.5a consent order directly to the registry. a litigant in person agrees to the sum proposed, and the other side assumes that agreement is sufficient for the paper procedure. a consent draft grants possession of a dwelling-house under the same route used for commercial premises. the draft contains only an extension for filing a pleading, with no order within r.5a(2)(a) or (b). a defendant offers narrower restraints, but the plaintiffs have not yet accepted the proposal."],["CIV-7.03","PCLL8010","LG7","drawing-up-orders","Drawing up, settling and perfecting an order","drawing up, settling and perfecting an order record what the court made, identify when it takes effect, and complete the approval and sealing process without inventing terms. a judge pronounces an order today but gives reasons later; a party says it has no effect until reasons are delivered. the applicant has done nothing to draw up the order for more than 7 days. the draft omits the affidavit tendered at the hearing and the ruling refusing leave to rely on another affirmation. a routine extension order contains special conditions, but the solicitor assumes it need not be drawn up. a clerk amends the draft and the solicitor disagrees with the change. a party treats sealing as the date from which every order first takes effect."],["CIV-7.04","PCLL8010","LG7","worked-injunction-summons","Worked drafting: employee restraints and a proposed consent order","worked drafting: employee restraints and a proposed consent order translate the employer’s and purchaser’s separate rights into immediate relief, then adapt the draft to the defendant’s limited concessions without inventing agreement or admissions. an employer and the purchaser of its group want immediate protection from a former executive who is allegedly competing and recruiting their staff. the statement of claim seeks final restraints, while the current instruction is to prevent further harm before trial. two plaintiffs seek similar restraints under different contracts, but the draft treats both as parties to the same agreement. the draft restrains use of confidential information without identifying the information in the supplied schedule. the proposed application repeats the final claim for damages and an account of profits as if those were the immediate protective order. a draft restraint expires in september 2024 although the application is made in december 2024 and employment ended only on 30 september 2024. the former executive accepts a restraint only until 30 september 2025, although the purchaser sought a longer period. the defendant denies taking trade secrets but agrees to comply with future confidentiality restraints. the defendant asks for 21 days to deliver up material, but the proposed draft retains an immediate delivery obligation. the instruction asks for standard costs terms without stating which costs order the parties have agreed. the draft recites admissions of theft although the defendant has expressly denied taking information. the defendant approves proposed terms, but no acceptance has been obtained from the plaintiffs."],["CIV-7.05","PCLL8010","LG7","security-gateways","Security for costs: gateways and ordinary residence","security for costs: gateways and ordinary residence identify the real plaintiff and the applicable o.23 ground, then prove ordinary residence from the individual’s life or the company’s actual management and control. a defendant fears an adverse costs award will be unrecoverable because the plaintiff lives abroad. an impecunious individual plaintiff lives in hong kong, gives a correct address and sues on his own claim. a person with no beneficial interest sues for somebody else and has no apparent means to meet costs. the plaintiff's address is omitted from the writ, but evidence shows an innocent clerical mistake. the plaintiff moves during the case and the defendant alleges that the move was intended to evade the litigation. a plaintiff seeks security from a foreign defendant merely because the defendant has issued an interlocutory summons. the plaintiff keeps a hong kong identity card but has moved the family home and employment abroad. the plaintiff has spent several months overseas for a temporary purpose and maintains ordinary life in hong kong. a bvi company sues in hong kong, and the defendant relies only on its place of incorporation. a company asserts that it is managed in hong kong but gives no evidence of who makes its decisions or where. the plaintiff is a non-trading holding company whose board and corporate activities are outside hong kong. an overseas-incorporated company is now managed by liquidators in hong kong, and the defendant assumes its residence cannot have changed."],["CIV-7.06","PCLL8010","LG7","security-discretion","Security discretion: merits, recovery, delay and stifling","security discretion: merits, recovery, delay and stifling after establishing a gateway, weigh practical recovery, clearly strong merits, timing and substantiated inability to pursue a genuine claim. a foreign plaintiff has substantial realisable assets in hong kong sufficient to meet the costs sought. the plaintiff relies on shares in a private company but gives no evidence of their value or ability to be realised. the defendant assumes enforcement abroad must be difficult without addressing the applicable reciprocal regime. a plaintiff resists security by calling its claim arguable, without showing a high probability of success. the documentary terms strongly support the plaintiff, which asks the court to refuse security on merits. the parties invite a lengthy trial of disputed evidence at the security hearing. d waits until the pre-trial review to seek substantial security despite knowing the relevant facts much earlier. p has already spent heavily preparing for trial and would have to abandon that work if security is demanded immediately. a security application was refused and d repeats it without identifying a substantial change. p says security will end the claim but gives no candid account of finances. a company has no cash itself but may be able to raise litigation funds elsewhere. p could provide funds but refuses to do so, describing the demand as stifling."],["CIV-7.07","PCLL8010","LG7","security-counterclaims","Co-plaintiffs, counterclaims and the real attacker","co-plaintiffs, counterclaims and the real attacker look beyond the party labels and ask which issues and costs will remain even if the claim is stopped. a foreign plaintiff sues with a hong kong co-plaintiff on the same cause of action. the local co-plaintiff is a thinly capitalised company with no apparent independent business. a defendant counterclaims on essentially the same facts and would litigate them even if the plaintiff's claim were stayed. a foreign defendant brings an independent cross-action concerning a separate transaction. d seeks security for expenditure which is also needed to prosecute its own counterclaim. a proposed default term would prevent p defending the counterclaim as well as pursuing its claim."],["CIV-7.08","PCLL8010","LG7","security-company","Security against a company under s905","security against a company under s905 use credible financial evidence to establish the corporate gateway, independently of where the company is ordinarily resident. a hong kong limited company has little apparent business and no evidence of assets sufficient to meet defence costs. an overseas-incorporated company is centrally managed in hong kong, so the residence-abroad gateway is disputed. d asserts that a company cannot pay but supplies no affidavit or financial material. a small issued share capital is treated as conclusive proof that the company has no resources. a foreign-incorporated company argues that central management in hong kong defeats every possible application for security. a company argues that local residence alone prevents any security order."],["CIV-7.09","PCLL8010","LG7","security-quantum-terms","Security: the amount, payment terms, stay and default","security: the amount, payment terms, stay and default turn a supported costs estimate into a proportionate order; do not mistake security for judgment on the claim or counterclaim. d asks for its full solicitor-and-client bill without allowing for taxation or early disposal. the estimate includes a trial which the proposed procedural route is unlikely to require. d seeks both past and future costs but gives no explanation of the stages to be secured. the draft requires payment within 18 hours although funding must be obtained elsewhere. the proposed order gives d judgment on the counterclaim automatically if security is not paid. the draft says security must be given but omits the amount, method, date and effect on proceedings."],["CIV-7.10","PCLL8010","LG7","security-appeal","Security for the costs of an appeal","security for the costs of an appeal apply o.59 r.10(5), identify special circumstances and support a proportionate estimate of appellate costs. the successful party below seeks protection for the costs of responding to an appeal by an impecunious appellant. an appellant lives abroad and the respondent identifies difficulty or delay in recovering appeal costs. the appellant has not paid a small previous costs award, but the evidence of actual means is incomplete. the respondent demands $300,000 for the appeal without an itemised estimate. an appellant relies only on having obtained leave as proof that security should be refused. the respondent applies directly without first writing to request security."],["CCT-2.01","PCLL8020","LG2","establishing-a-company","Establishing or repurposing a company","establishing or repurposing a company client needs a hong kong company — incorporate a new one, activate a shelf company, or incorporate an existing business. the client wants to be trading by the end of the month and asks whether to incorporate fresh or buy a shelf company. a shelf company has been bought and the subscriber shares still stand in the nominee's name. the client has been running the business as a sole trader for six years and now wants it in a company. the incorporation form has gone in and the client asks what still has to happen before the company can open a bank account. the client proposes a name made up of two english words and a chinese character. the company has an english name and a chinese name, and the seal being engraved shows only the english one. the company has moved office and 20 days have passed since the move. the company keeps its statutory books at its accountants' office in kwun tong rather than at the registered office. the shareholders resolved on 3 june to change the name and the client wants to sign contracts in the new name that week. a change-of-name special resolution has been passed and the client asks what has to be filed and by when. the business being transferred into the company has trade creditors, and the parties are deciding whether the company takes on the liabilities. the first board meeting has been convened and the agenda lists only the appointment of auditors."],["CCT-2.02","PCLL8020","LG2","articles-and-constitution","Articles of association","articles of association what must be in the articles, when the model articles apply, what the articles bind, and how to alter them. the company was incorporated with no registered articles at all, and the question is what governs it. the articles attached to the facts replace ma11(2) and fix the board quorum at 3. the articles attached to the facts have ma17 deleted in its entirety. the company was incorporated in 1998 and its articles have never been amended. the articles of a limited company say nothing about the liability of its members. the company holds a licence to dispense with \"limited\" and its articles state no objects. a member sues another member to enforce a pre-emption provision in the articles, and the company is not a party. a member has not paid the call due under the articles and the company wants to sue for it. the articles require a special resolution to remove a director, and the shareholders pass an ordinary one. the shareholders want to raise the maximum number of shares the company may issue, and propose a special resolution. the articles were altered on 1 may and nothing has been delivered to the registrar."],["CCT-2.03","PCLL8020","LG2","directors-office","Directors: appointment, retirement and removal","directors: appointment, retirement and removal who can be a director, how they get in, and how they get out — including removal by ordinary resolution and the rights that go with it. the proposed director turns 18 next month and the board wants to appoint him now. the proposed director was made bankrupt two years ago and has not been discharged. the company is a private company and every one of its directors is a body corporate. the board appointed a new director in march and the client asks how long he holds office for. the company has dispensed with agms and a board-appointed director wants to know when he must retire. the sole member of a private company is also its sole director, and he wants cover if he dies. a director has been absent from board meetings for seven months without asking anyone's permission. a director hands in a resignation letter at the office although the articles require written notice. a director has resigned and believes the company will not notify the registrar. the majority shareholder wants a director out and the articles require a special resolution. the director to be removed has held office for life since before 31 august 1984. the shareholders want to remove a director by written resolution to avoid a meeting. the director's shares carry ten votes each on a resolution to remove him. the company receives notice of the removal resolution and does not send a copy to the director. the director sends written representations and the company circulates none of them. two directors' appointments are proposed at the same meeting and each wants to vote on the other's. a director was appointed on 2 april and nothing has been filed."],["CCT-2.04","PCLL8020","LG2","board-decision-making","Getting a valid Board decision","getting a valid board decision convening, notice, mode, quorum and voting — and what changes in a sole-, single- or multi-director company. the board wants to allot new shares and the shareholders have given no approval. the shareholders pass a special resolution directing the directors not to sell the kowloon premises. the board wants to delegate the day-to-day running of a project to one director, in shanghai. a director in london telephones two colleagues separately, and each is told the same proposal. a director asks the company secretary to send out notice of a board meeting. the notice of the board meeting gives the date and time but not the place. notice of the board meeting was given orally to each director. two directors meet by chance in a lift, agree a course of action, and treat that as a board decision. the articles fix the quorum at 3, two directors attend, and they want to call another meeting. the chairperson has not joined the video call 12 minutes after the start time. the vote is 2-2 and the chairperson is an interested director not counted for quorum. one director is an alternate for two absent appointors, both of whom could have voted. all the directors except the interested one agree in writing, and together they would not make a quorum. the company has one director and articles that do not require more, and he decides to sell the main asset. the sole director decides on 1 june and provides the company with a written record on 20 june. the company has three directors, two have resigned, and the quorum is 2. a decision taken last year turns out to have been taken by someone whose appointment was defective."],["CCT-2.05","PCLL8020","LG2","directors-interests","Director's interest in a transaction (s536 / MA16)","director's interest in a transaction (s536 / ma16) the three elements of the disclosure duty, how and when to declare, and what ma16 then stops the director doing. the company is about to lease premises from a company in which a director holds 40% of the shares. a director's wife owns the supplier the company proposes to contract with. the transaction is worth $30,000 to a company with a $50m turnover. the director says he had no idea his brother-in-law controlled the counterparty, though the shareholding is on a public register. the interest concerns the terms of the director's own service contract, being considered by the board. the director told the board he \"has an interest\" in the counterparty and said nothing more. the declaration made last month has since become inaccurate as the shareholding has changed. the company has already signed the contract and the director has declared nothing. the director sends a written declaration to the other directors by email, which they have agreed to accept. the director gives a general notice about a body corporate but does not state the extent of his interest. the interested director votes anyway and the resolution passes 3-2 including his vote. the arrangement is the company giving the director security for money he lent it. the arrangement is an employee benefit scheme that gives the directors better terms than the staff. the arrangement is an agreement to underwrite an issue of the company's shares. an alternate acts for two appointors, one interested and one not. with the interested director excluded, only one director remains and the quorum is 2. the company is a one-member company, that member is also a director, and the contract is oral and outside the ordinary course of business. the director declared nothing and now relies on ma17 to keep the profit."],["CCT-2.06","PCLL8020","LG2","company-secretary","Company secretary","company secretary the residence requirement, the three restrictions on wearing both hats, and who can act when the office is vacant. the company secretary is a natural person who lives and works in shenzhen. the company secretary is a body corporate incorporated in the bvi with no office in hong kong. the incorporation form named a firm as company secretary, and the client asks who actually holds the office. the company has one director and he proposes to be the company secretary as well. the company has two directors, one of whom is also the company secretary, and a document needs a director and the secretary. the articles are said to bar the arrangement even though the statute does not. the board wants to remove the company secretary it appointed last year. the company secretary is on leave and a deputy signs in her place. the office of secretary is vacant and the directors authorise an officer to act. a new company secretary was appointed on 1 march and no nd2a has been filed."],["CCT-2.07","PCLL8020","LG2","executing-documents","Executing corporate documents","executing corporate documents under hand, under seal, or as a deed — who must sign, and what the board has to authorise first. the document is a guarantee that has to be executed as a deed. the contract would have to be in writing and signed if made between individuals, and a manager proposes to sign it. the manager signs and nobody can point to any board resolution authorising him. the company has adopted a common seal and the secretary affixes it without any board decision. the board has decided that sealed documents need no counter-signature, and the document carries the seal alone. the sealed document is signed by two directors and no authorised person. the company secretary signs a sealed document as the authorised person alongside a director. the company has a seal but the parties prefer not to use it on this document. the company has only one director, and he signs alone under s127(3). the company has four directors and two of them sign. one person is both the sole director and the company secretary, and signs in both capacities. a share certificate is to be issued and the question is how it may be executed."],["CCT-3.01","PCLL8020","LG3","funding-debt-or-equity","Funding: debt or equity, and shareholders' loans","funding: debt or equity, and shareholders' loans how the company raises money, and what changes depending on whether it is a loan or a share issue. the company needs $2m and the two existing shareholders are willing to put it in themselves. a shareholder advances $500,000 with no fixed repayment date, no interest and no security. a shareholder wants a return on the money he puts in whether or not there are distributable profits. the company is short of cash and a shareholder who lent it money wants to be repaid ahead of the trade creditors. an outside investor wants a say in how the business is run as the price of his money. the buyer of the shares also wants the benefit of the seller's outstanding loan to the company. the assignment of the shareholder's loan is of half the outstanding balance only. the deed of assignment is signed by the assignor company but nobody has told the company that owes the money. the assignor is a company and the assignment is executed as a deed without being expressed to be one. the shareholder wants his money back and the company has no distributable profits."],["CCT-3.02","PCLL8020","LG3","share-capital-and-value","Share capital, share value and distributions","share capital, share value and distributions registering and altering share capital, working out what a share is worth, and when a dividend may lawfully be paid. the company was incorporated with 100 shares at $1 and is now worth $8m; the question is what a share is worth. the directors are fixing a subscription price for new shares and have not established the company's value. the company owns a flat worth $12m, owes the bank $5m and owes a shareholder $2m. the board wants to capitalise profits and issue bonus shares without increasing share capital. the shareholders want to convert every $1 share into ten 10-cent shares. the company wants to cancel 5,000 shares that a subscriber agreed to take but never paid for. shares were cancelled a fortnight ago and the directors did it without any resolution of the company. shares were converted on 1 march and nothing has been delivered to the registrar. the company allotted shares last week and the secretary is about to file form nsc11. the directors want to pay an interim dividend and the members have passed no resolution. the members at the agm want to declare a larger dividend than the directors recommended. the company has accumulated realised profits of $3m and accumulated realised losses of $4m, and wants to pay a dividend. the company proposes to distribute a piece of land rather than cash. a dividend turns out to have been paid unlawfully, and one member had no idea of the company's position. the directors want to know how much of the company's worth they can safely pay out."],["CCT-3.03","PCLL8020","LG3","share-ownership-nominee","Registered, beneficial and nominee ownership","registered, beneficial and nominee ownership splitting legal from beneficial ownership, and the three documents a nominee arrangement needs. the client wants to own the shares but not appear on any public register as holding them. the register of members names the nominee and the beneficial owner asks why the certificate is not in his name. the company is asked to note on its register that the shares are held on trust. the beneficial owner wants to be able to sell the shares later without going back to the nominee. the nominee has been handed an instrument of transfer with the transferee, the consideration and the date left blank. the nominee proposes to vote at the general meeting as he thinks best. a dividend is declared and the nominee asks who is entitled to it. the declaration of trust has been executed and the parties want evidence of the date it was made. the shares are held by a nominee and the significant controllers register has to be completed. the nominee has been asked to transfer the shares and wants to know whether he may refuse."],["CCT-3.04","PCLL8020","LG3","shareholder-decision-making","Getting a valid shareholders' approval","getting a valid shareholders' approval which matters are reserved to shareholders, and how an agm, a general meeting or a written resolution is validly done. the board wants to allot shares to a new investor and no members' resolution has been passed. the company's financial year ended on 31 december and the agm has not been held by the following october. notice of the agm was given 18 days before the meeting. the company wants to hold the agm on 10 days' notice and one member out of five objects. members holding 6% of the paid-up capital want a general meeting called. the members required a meeting on 1 april and the directors have called nothing by 25 april. the notice convening the general meeting sets it down for 40 days after the date of the notice. a special resolution is to be proposed and the notice describes it but does not set out its text. the notice was served on 1 june for a meeting on 22 june, and the drafter counted both days. short notice is proposed and the agreeing members are a majority in number holding 90% of the votes. the notice went to every member but not to the auditor. one member's notice went astray in the post and the meeting went ahead. a member wants to attend the meeting from vancouver by video link. a member appoints two proxies and both want to vote on a show of hands. a corporate member sends its company secretary along without any formal authorisation. only one member turns up to the meeting of a two-member company. the chairperson of the board has not arrived 20 minutes after the start time. the meeting is inquorate and the members present want to transact the one item on the agenda. the resolution requires special notice and the members want to pass it in writing."],["CCT-3.05","PCLL8020","LG3","share-allotment","Allotment of new shares","allotment of new shares new shares created by the company — and whether the offer falls inside a s140(2) exception, which decides if the members must approve first. the board proposes to allot 10,000 new shares to an incoming investor, and the members have approved nothing. the offer of new shares is made to all the existing members in proportion to their holdings. new shares are being issued as a bonus issue to the members pro rata. a founder member is being allotted the shares he agreed in the articles to take. a member's registered address is in a country where the offer may not lawfully be made. the members gave a general approval at last year's agm and this year's agm was held in june. the members approved the allotment after the board had already resolved on it. the directors propose to allot at par when the company is worth many times that. the shares are to be allotted in satisfaction of a debt the company owes the subscriber. the allottee has paid but nobody has entered his name in the register of members. the allotment was made on 1 april and no certificate has been issued by 20 june. the allotment takes the new shareholder above 25% of the company. the allotment was made a fortnight ago and no return has been delivered to the registrar. the certificate issued covers shares of two different classes."],["CCT-3.06","PCLL8020","LG3","share-transfer","Transfer of existing shares","transfer of existing shares existing shares moving from an existing holder — stamping first, then the board, then the register. a shareholder is selling his holding and the buyer wants to know when he actually becomes the owner. the parties have named the company itself as transferor in the instrument of transfer. the transferor is the registered holder but the shares are beneficially owned by someone else. the transferee is a company and the instrument is signed by its general manager. the shareholders' agreement gives the other members a right of first refusal that nobody has offered. the articles are silent on restricting transfers in a private company. the instrument of transfer is executed by the transferor only. the company proposes to charge a $500 fee for registering the transfer. the shares are being given to the transferor's son for no consideration, and the parties ask about contract notes. the instrument of transfer has been lodged with the company unstamped. the transfer was lodged on 1 february and by 20 april the board has neither registered it nor refused. the board refuses to register and the transferee asks why. the transferee asked for reasons on 1 may and has heard nothing by 10 june. the directors suspect the transfer is fraudulent and are holding on to the instrument. the board's refusal looks arbitrary and the transferee wants the court to order registration. the transfer was registered and the old share certificates were never returned for cancellation. the seller also lent the company $300,000 and the sale agreement says nothing about it. the transfer changes who holds more than 25% of the company. a shareholder has died and his executor wants the shares registered in his name."],["CCT-3.07","PCLL8020","LG3","corporate-maintenance","Registers, records and Companies Registry filings","registers, records and companies registry filings the registers a company must keep, the significant controllers test, and what gets filed when. the company has moved its registered office and the client asks what has to be filed and when. the statutory books are kept at the director's home in sha tin. a new director was appointed three weeks ago and nothing has been filed. a director has changed his residential address. the company allotted shares last month and the return has not gone in. the company converted its shares and the secretary is unsure which form to use. a share transfer was registered in march and the client asks when the registrar is told. the annual return is due and the client thinks stating the address there covers the change of registered office. the company has never kept a register of significant controllers. minutes of board meetings from eight years ago are about to be destroyed. the articles were altered by special resolution and only the resolution has been filed."],["CCT-4.01","PCLL8020","LG4","hong-kong-stock","Is it Hong Kong stock at all?","is it hong kong stock at all? the gateway question — stamp duty on a share transfer only bites on \"hong kong stock\", and loan capital is outside \"stock\" entirely. the shares being transferred are in a company incorporated in the bvi whose register of members is kept in hong kong. the shares are in a hong kong incorporated private company and both parties are in singapore. what is being assigned is the shareholder's loan to the company, not the shares. the company is issuing 100 new shares to an incoming investor and the client asks about stamp duty. the parties assume duty is payable because value is passing, without pointing to any charging provision. the register of members of the target is kept in bermuda. a share-for-share swap is proposed and the question is whether anything is stampable at all. the client has been told \"it's a share deal, so it's 0.2%\" and wants that checked."],["CCT-4.02","PCLL8020","LG4","which-charging-head","Which charging head applies?","which charging head applies? the order of analysis that decides everything else — and the three heads it routes to. shares worth $5m are being sold for $5m in cash between unconnected parties. shares worth $5m are being transferred for $1m, and the buyer is the seller's nephew. shares are being transferred for no consideration at all as a birthday gift. the price is $2m and the buyer is also taking an assignment of the company's $3m debt to the seller. the buyer is guaranteeing the target's bank borrowings as part of the deal. the transfer is from a nominee back to the beneficial owner. the transfer is between two companies both 95% owned by the same holding company. the duty as calculated comes to $4,213.60. the parties have prepared an instrument of transfer and no contract notes. the client wants to know which documents get stamped and at what rate before anything is signed."],["CCT-4.03","PCLL8020","LG4","sale-or-purchase","Sale or purchase: contract notes and s19","sale or purchase: contract notes and s19 what counts as a sale or purchase, the three things s19(1) requires, and what a contract note must contain. the parties exchange shares in one company for shares in another and say no money changed hands. the company allots new shares to a subscriber for cash, and the parties prepare contract notes. shares are sold for $800,000 and nobody has made a bought note or a sold note. the contract notes were made and stamped, and nothing was endorsed on the instrument of transfer. the contract note gives the date of the transaction but not the date of settlement. the broker signs the contract note without saying whether he acts as principal or agent. the contract note describes the stock but gives no price per unit. the agreed price is $1m and the shares were worth $3m on the day of the sale. the board is asked to register the transfer and the parties produce only the stamped contract notes. the client wants to know why there are three documents rather than one."],["CCT-4.04","PCLL8020","LG4","deemed-consideration","Deemed consideration under s24","deemed consideration under s24 when a payment the transfer is subject to, or indebtedness the transferee takes on, is added to the stampable consideration. the buyer pays $1 for the shares and $4m to take an assignment of the seller's loan to the target. the buyer pays the agreed price for the shares and separately, on unconnected terms, buys a debt owed by a different company. the sale agreement makes the share transfer conditional on the loan assignment completing at the same time. the buyer instructs its parent company to pay the seller's creditor directly. the buyer undertakes to procure the target to repay its $6m bank debt within a year. the buyer guarantees the target's overdraft as part of the deal. the buyer agrees to provide funds if the target defaults on its borrowings. the debt being assigned has a face value of $5m and the buyer pays $1m for it. the debt the buyer takes on is almost certainly irrecoverable. the shares are being gifted and the recipient also takes over a loan. the deemed consideration works out at $2m and the shares are worth $6m. the instrument of transfer says nothing about the loan assignment that accompanied it."],["CCT-4.05","PCLL8020","LG4","voluntary-disposition","Transfers operating as a voluntary disposition","transfers operating as a voluntary disposition gifts and undervalue transfers — the two-part s27(4) test, head 2(3), and mandatory adjudication. a father transfers 200 shares to his daughter for $1. shares with a net asset value of $4m are transferred for $500,000 between business partners. the consideration is below value but the transferee is giving up something substantial in return. the target company owns two flats in kowloon and the parties are assembling the valuation evidence. the target was incorporated three weeks ago and has never traded. the shares are listed and the transfer is dated a monday after a public holiday. the parties have calculated 0.2% on the $500,000 they actually paid. the parties have stamped head 2(3) and are about to add $5 under head 2(4) as well. the parties have prepared contract notes for the gift. the instrument has been stamped and nobody has applied for adjudication. the client asks whether the $50 adjudication fee is payable."],["CCT-4.06","PCLL8020","LG4","nominee-exemption","Nominee arrangements: the s27(5) exemption","nominee arrangements: the s27(5) exemption setting up, changing and unwinding a nominee holding — and why the declaration of trust must be adjudicated at the time. the nominee is retiring and the shares are moving to a new nominee. the nominee is transferring the shares back to the beneficial owner. a liquidator is distributing shares in specie to the members. shares are being transferred to a lender as security for a loan. the parties expect the whole transfer to be free of duty and are surprised by the $5. the instrument of transfer says nothing about the circumstances of the nominee arrangement. the declaration of trust was executed five years ago and never taken to the stamp office. the declaration of trust was adjudicated as not stampable at the time it was executed. the stamp office refuses the exemption and proposes to stamp the transfer at head 2(3). the beneficial owner bought the shares two years ago and only now wants them put into a nominee's name. the beneficial owner holds a transfer endorsed in blank and wants to move the shares without contacting the outgoing nominee."],["CCT-4.07","PCLL8020","LG4","intra-group-relief","Intra-group relief under s45","intra-group relief under s45 transfers between associated bodies corporate — the 90% test, the three disqualifying conditions, and withdrawal within two years. shares are being transferred between two subsidiaries, each 95% owned by the same holding company. a co owns 95% of b co, b co owns 91% of c co, and the transfer is from a co to c co. a co owns 90% of b co and 90% of c co, and the transfer is between b co and c co. the two companies are owned outright by the same married couple. the transferor is incorporated in the cayman islands and the transferee in hong kong. the consideration for the intra-group transfer is being provided by an outside investor. the beneficial interest was acquired from a third party outside the group last month. the transferee is to be sold out of the group shortly after the transfer. relief was claimed 18 months ago and the transferee is now being sold. the group ceased to be associated on 1 march and nothing has been notified to the stamp office. the relief is being claimed and no statutory declaration has been prepared. the transfer has been routed through an interposed company so that each link is 90% or more."],["CCT-4.08","PCLL8020","LG4","time-limits-and-methods","Time limits, methods and what to lodge","time limits, methods and what to lodge the three stamping deadlines, how to stamp, and the documents the stamp office needs to assess consideration against value. the sale and purchase was effected in hong kong on monday and the contract notes are still unstamped on friday. the sale and purchase was effected in shanghai and the client asks how long he has. a gift of shares was executed in hong kong on 1 march and the parties plan to stamp on 20 march. an ordinary transfer is about to be executed in hong kong and the parties intend to stamp it afterwards. the instrument was executed in vancouver and the parties are counting from the date of the agreement. the parties want to e-stamp a transfer where the buyer is taking on the target's indebtedness. four thousand instruments have to be stamped for a group reorganisation. the consideration is subject to a post-completion adjustment. the target was incorporated 14 months ago and the parties are gathering evidence of value. the latest audited accounts are made up to a date 11 months before the transfer. a dividend was declared after the date of the latest audited accounts. the target owns a warehouse in yuen long and no irsd102 has been prepared. there is no sale and purchase agreement and the stamp office wants to know that."],["CCT-4.09","PCLL8020","LG4","non-stamping-and-avoidance","Non-stamping, penalties, evasion and avoidance","non-stamping, penalties, evasion and avoidance what an unstamped instrument cannot do, the late-stamping penalty scale, and the line between avoidance and evasion. the transfer was never stamped and the transferee wants to sue on it. the instrument is unstamped and the board is asked to register the transferee. a dividend is about to be paid on shares whose transfer was never duly stamped. the transferee has not been registered and assumes he owns the shares anyway. stamping is three weeks late. stamping is seven weeks late. stamping is five months late. the client wants the penalty remitted and has a genuine explanation for the delay. the instrument understates the consideration and the parties knew it. the parties left the loan assignment out of the documents shown to the stamp office. a two-step transfer through an interposed company keeps each link above the s45 threshold. the client asks where the line is between arranging affairs to pay less duty and something worse."],["CCT-5.01","PCLL8020","LG5","problem-solving-process","The problem-solving method","the problem-solving method the six-stage process for turning a client's goal into an action plan — and the structure to write an answer in. the client says what she wants to end up with and the instructions do not say how the company is owned. the client's stated goal cannot lawfully be achieved in the form he has asked for. you need to know the issued share capital and the directors before advising, and the client cannot tell you. two routes both achieve the goal, at different cost and on different timetables. you have formed a clear view on which route is better and have not yet heard from the client. the client has chosen a route and asks what happens, in what order, and by when. the transaction has a statutory deadline and the steps have not been sequenced against it. the client wants to be told what to do, and the draft advice recites the relevant sections instead."],["CCT-5.02","PCLL8020","LG5","allotment-or-transfer","Worked example: allotment or transfer?","worked example: allotment or transfer? the lg5 illustration — two shareholders want to give their daughter 100 shares, and the method decides how. a couple own all 1,000 shares between them and want their daughter to have 100 as a birthday present. the parents want to share the gift equally, and the question is what that means in documents. new shares are to go to one person only, so the offer is not in proportion to existing holdings. the gift is to be made by transfer and the parties ask what stamp duty is payable. the gift is to be made by allotment and the parties ask what stamp duty is payable. the client asks how the two routes differ in their effect on the existing shareholdings. the company has adopted the model articles unmodified and has a common seal, and new certificates must be executed. the mother holds 80% before the transaction and the significant controllers register is being checked. the subscription price has to be fixed and nobody has valued the company. the client wants to know which route involves filing something at the companies registry."],["CCT-5.03","PCLL8020","LG5","practice-scenarios","The two practice scenarios","the two practice scenarios what the two lg5 corporate practice problems put in issue — attempt them before the solutions recording. you are working through the lg5 practice problems before the solutions recording. a scenario hands you model articles with an attachment replacing ma7(3), ma11(2) or ma16. a director has been absent from board meetings for six weeks and cannot return. a majority shareholder is also a passive 55% owner of the company bidding for the contract. a sale must complete in 10 days and one of the two directors is overseas. shares are sold at market value together with a shareholder's loan assigned dollar-for-dollar. you have to give the parties to each step, put the steps in order, and price the duty and the filings for each."],["CCT-6.01","PCLL8020","LG6","sha-nature-and-parties","What an SHA is, and who is bound by it","what an sha is, and who is bound by it an sha is a contract, so it binds only its parties — which makes \"who signed, and in what capacity\" the first question in every sha problem. a third party has just acquired shares from an existing shareholder and has not signed anything beyond the sale and purchase agreement. a shareholder who is not a party to the sha refuses to vote the way the other shareholders say the agreement requires. the company itself is named as a party to the sha and has undertaken not to allot further shares without unanimous consent. the sha was signed by two of the three shareholders, and the third now proposes to do the very thing the agreement prohibits. the company has allotted shares in breach of an sha undertaking and the other shareholders want the allotment set aside. a transferee has taken shares and the remaining shareholder wants to hold him to the transfer restrictions in clause 8. two shareholders signed the sha, a third did not, and the two now want to enforce the reserved matters against the third."],["CCT-6.02","PCLL8020","LG6","sha-articles-and-co","SHA, articles and the Ordinance: which governs?","sha, articles and the ordinance: which governs? the three overlap. separate the question of substance (can the sha change the rule?) from the question of procedure (the sha gives you the right; the articles still tell you how to exercise it). a shareholder has increased its holding from 15% to 25% and gives written notice to the other shareholder and the company secretary that it is appointing a director. the sha fixes a board quorum different from the one in the articles, and a meeting has been held on the sha's footing. a resolution has been passed in accordance with the articles but in breach of an undertaking in the sha. the parties want a provision kept out of the public domain and are deciding whether to put it in the articles or the sha. the sha is to be amended and one shareholder objects that a special resolution would be needed. the company has only one director and the sha assumes there are four. a clause of the sha purports to remove a matter from the shareholders in general meeting altogether."],["CCT-6.03","PCLL8020","LG6","sha-board-composition","Board composition and board control","board composition and board control who gets to put directors on the board, what makes a meeting quorate, and who wins a vote — clause 3 against the model articles default. a shareholder holding 25% of the issued share capital wants a seat on a board the 75% holder otherwise controls. the minority's nominated director has stopped attending board meetings and the majority wants to press on without him. the board is split two votes to two and the chairperson purports to exercise a casting vote. a shareholder's holding has fallen below 25% after a share issue and its appointed director has not resigned. a board meeting was held with only the majority's three nominees present and resolved to borrow hk$5 million. the company has four directors, three appointed by the 75% holder and one by the 25% holder, and a resolution passed three to one. a shareholder purports to remove its nominated director by letter to the company secretary alone."],["CCT-6.04","PCLL8020","LG6","sha-reserved-matters","Reserved Matters: the consent threshold","reserved matters: the consent threshold clause 5 takes a list of decisions away from the ordinary machinery and makes them conditional on the written consent of every shareholder with 25% or more. the board has resolved to borrow hk$8 million from a third party and the 25% shareholder was not asked. the company proposes to sell its principal operating asset and the majority says the board alone can decide it. a shareholder holding 24% complains that its consent was not obtained for an alteration of the articles. the articles are to be altered and a special resolution has been passed by the 75% holder alone. the company wants to change the business it carries on and the sha describes the business by reference to a defined term. a shareholder was diluted from 25% to 14.29% and the majority now proposes a members' voluntary winding-up. the majority argues that a reserved matter is a board matter and so outside the shareholders' consent requirement."],["CCT-6.05","PCLL8020","LG6","sha-scope-and-conduct","Scope of business and conduct of the company's affairs","scope of business and conduct of the company's affairs clauses 2 and 4: what the company is for, and the running undertakings about how it will be operated while the agreement lasts. the company has started a second line of business that the majority says is a natural extension of the first. no board meeting has been held for four months and the minority's nominated director has been asking for one. the minority shareholder has received no management accounts since the company was formed. a payment was made out of the company's bank account on the signature of one director without any board resolution about how the account is operated. the majority proposes that the company should also act as guarantor for an affiliate's borrowings. the company's accounts have been prepared on a basis the minority says is not generally accepted in hong kong. the parties disagree about whether an activity falls within the defined business."],["CCT-6.06","PCLL8020","LG6","sha-future-financing","Future financing, and the dilution that follows","future financing, and the dilution that follows clause 6 sets the order — banks first, then a call on the shareholders — and clause 6.2(b) decides what happens to a shareholder who will not or cannot pay. the company needs further working capital and the board has issued a subscription notice without first approaching any bank. a subscription notice required the 75% holder to subscribe for 75 shares and the 25% holder for 25 shares at $1 each within 30 days, and the minority will not subscribe. a bank has offered the funding the company needs on condition that it receives a small equity stake. a shareholder subscribed on the thirty-second day after the date of the subscription notice. the majority wants to take up the shares the minority has declined. a shareholder who failed to subscribe is now told it has committed a material breach and must transfer all its shares. the subscription notice calls for an amount larger than the maximum aggregate figure stated in the agreement. after a share issue a shareholder's holding has fallen from 25% to 14.29% and it wants to know what it has lost."],["CCT-6.07","PCLL8020","LG6","sha-dividend-policy","Dividend policy","dividend policy clause 7 turns the board's discretion over distributions into a contractual floor, without displacing the statutory limits on what may be paid. the sha requires at least 40% of distributable profits to be distributed and the board has recommended no dividend for the year. the minority shareholder has received no return on its investment for three years while the company has been profitable. the board has paid an interim dividend that the minority says should have been put to the general meeting. the general meeting has purported to declare a dividend larger than the board recommended. the company has cash but no profits available for distribution, and the sha sets a distribution percentage. the majority wants to retain everything as working capital and the sha fixes a distribution floor."],["CCT-6.08","PCLL8020","LG6","sha-voluntary-transfer","Voluntary transfer and the right of first refusal","voluntary transfer and the right of first refusal clause 8: a general prohibition, then a pre-emption process whose most examined feature is that a notice to sell part offers the whole. a shareholder holding 75 of the 100 issued shares wishes to sell 35 of them to a named third party. the outgoing shareholder wishes to sell half its holding and has served a transfer notice specifying only those shares. the remaining shareholder has asked the company's auditors to determine a price and has then let the period run out without accepting. a transfer notice was served and the outgoing shareholder now wants to withdraw it. the remaining shareholder served an acceptance notice 45 days ago and completion has not happened. a shareholder has granted a charge over its shares to its bank without asking anyone. the transfer price determined by the auditors is lower than the price the outgoing shareholder originally asked for. a third-party sale completed 70 days after the acceptance period expired. the transferee has signed everything and the board is refusing to register the transfer."],["CCT-6.09","PCLL8020","LG6","sha-compulsory-transfer","Compulsory transfer on an Event of Default","compulsory transfer on an event of default clause 9 lists what counts as a default and then hands the non-defaulting shareholder the power to force the defaulter out at a valuation it cannot set. a shareholder has breached a material obligation and has done nothing about it for a month after being asked in writing to put it right. a receiver has been appointed over a substantial part of a corporate shareholder's undertaking and remains in place five weeks later. an individual shareholder has died and the other shareholder wants to buy out the estate. a corporate shareholder has been wound up by order of the court. the non-defaulting shareholder learned of a default three months after it happened and now wants to act. a shareholder has ceased to carry on a material part of its own business. the defaulting shareholder says it will serve a transfer notice at its own asking price. the non-defaulting shareholder gave notice 25 business days after becoming aware of the default."],["CCT-6.10","PCLL8020","LG6","sha-indirect-transfers","Direct and indirect transfers","direct and indirect transfers clause 8 catches a disposal of the shares themselves. a sale further up the ownership chain escapes it — and lands instead in clause 9.1(f). the shareholder of record is a holding company, and its own parent has agreed to sell that holding company to a third party. the ultimate individual owner proposes to sell his shares in the topmost company in the chain. a shareholder says the pre-emption clause is not engaged because it is not selling any shares in the company. the other shareholder complains that control of its co-owner has changed hands without any transfer notice. a corporate shareholder has replaced its entire board following a sale further up the group. the buyer of the parent company says it has not become a shareholder and so cannot be required to sign a deed of adherence."],["CCT-6.11","PCLL8020","LG6","sha-conflicting-provisions","Conflicting provisions: the agreement prevails","conflicting provisions: the agreement prevails clause 10 makes the sha prevail over the articles as between the shareholders, and obliges them to amend the articles so the conflict goes away. the articles give the chairperson a casting vote and the sha says there is none, and a board resolution has been carried on the chairperson's second vote. the articles fix a board quorum of two and the sha requires a director appointed by each shareholder. a resolution has been passed strictly in accordance with the articles and in breach of the sha. the parties have never amended the articles and the sha has been in force for three years. a new shareholder who signed a deed of adherence argues that the articles govern because they are the company's constitution. one shareholder refuses to vote in favour of the article amendments the other says the agreement requires."],["PRP-1.01","PCLL8030","LG1","what-is-land","Is it \"land\"? Fixtures, chattels and fittings","is it \"land\"? fixtures, chattels and fittings the s2 definition, the purpose-and-degree test, and the threefold classification elitestone added. the sale includes a conservatory built onto the back of the house and the buyer assumes it passes with the property. the vendor removes the ceiling lights and the chandelier the day before completion. a window-type air conditioner has been fitted into an aperture cut in the ballroom wall. a split air-conditioning system is ducted through the building, and the vendor says it is his to take. an air conditioner sits on the floor of a restaurant, plugged in and not fixed to anything. the purchaser wants to know what, precisely, he is getting for his money. a structure rests on the land by its own weight and has never been bolted down. the dispute is over an undivided share in a plot rather than a physical parcel. the item was annexed to the building for the better enjoyment of the item itself, not of the building."],["PRP-1.02","PCLL8030","LG1","boundaries-and-plans","Boundaries, plans and the verbal description","boundaries, plans and the verbal description there are no guaranteed boundaries in hong kong — so the question is one of construction, and the plan usually loses. the verbal description in the government lease gives frontages of 110 feet, and the annexed plan shows a strip several feet narrower. the parcels clause says the land is \"more particularly delineated and described\" on the annexed plan. two neighbouring owners each claim the same three feet of a new territories lot. the lot was sectioned and no plan was drawn up by a surveyor or registered. the land is in the new territories and the only survey is the cadastral one from 1893-1903. a purchaser asks whether he can rely on the government's plan as a guarantee of the area he is buying. the area is stated as \"1,815 square feet or thereabouts\" and the actual measurement differs. the words of the grant plainly contradict the plan and one of them has to give way."],["PRP-1.03","PCLL8030","LG1","sectioning-and-subdivision","Dividing a lot: sectioning and undivided shares","dividing a lot: sectioning and undivided shares two different ways to split a lot — physically by deed poll, or notionally into undivided shares — and they produce very different ownership. an owner of lot 1234 wants to sell off the eastern half of the plot and keep the rest. the title documents refer to \"section a of lot 1234\" and \"the remaining portion of lot 1234\" and the purchaser cannot follow the naming. a developer proposes to build a block of forty flats and sell each one separately. the purchaser of a flat asks what exactly he will own, given that the building sits on one lot. a flat owner claims to be entitled to possession of the whole lot because he is a tenant in common of it. section a has been sectioned again and the parties are unsure what the two new parts are called. the lot was divided and no surveyor's plan was registered. forty flats are about to go into separate hands and nobody has settled who will manage the building."],["PRP-1.04","PCLL8030","LG1","lease-or-conditions","Government lease or Conditions? Legal estate or equitable interest","government lease or conditions? legal estate or equitable interest which document made the grant decides whether your client holds a legal estate or merely an equitable interest — and what has to happen to convert it. the title bundle starts with a document headed \"conditions of sale\" and there is no government lease anywhere in it. the purchaser signed a memorandum of agreement at the fall of the hammer and paid a deposit at the auction. the building covenant required completion by 30 june 1989 and the building went up in 1988. land was granted in exchange for other land the government took back. land was granted at a reduced premium for a church, a school or a clinic. the client's government lease expired and he applied for a fresh grant. an assignment was executed after the conditions of sale but before any government lease was deemed issued. a mortgage registered between the conditions and the deemed lease is said to have lapsed on the deemed issue. the grantee has not yet complied with the positive conditions and wants to assign. the vendor claims to hold a legal estate under conditions dated 1976 and cannot produce a certificate of compliance."],["PRP-1.05","PCLL8030","LG1","certificate-of-compliance","Proving compliance and the 1 January 1970 divide","proving compliance and the 1 january 1970 divide whether the owner must prove compliance at all depends on one date — and where he must, the certificate has to be registered. the conditions of grant are dated 1976 and the vendor produces no certificate of compliance. a certificate of compliance was issued but never registered in the land registry. the conditions are dated 1965 and the purchaser's solicitor raises a requisition asking for the certificate. the certificate says compliance is confirmed \"without prejudice to all or any rights of the crown\" in respect of breaches existing or occurring later. the building covenant required completion by a stated date and the director inspected the lot afterwards. the developer obtained separate certificates for each section of a phased development. the vendor says the lot was inspected and everything was in order, but has nothing in writing. the purchaser wants to know whether the vendor now holds a legal estate at all."],["PRP-1.06","PCLL8030","LG1","term-renewal-extension","Term, renewal and extension","term, renewal and extension how long the grant runs, what 1997 did to it, and what the 2024 ordinance does about 2047. the government lease is for 999 years from 7 january 1862 at an annual rent of $20. the conditions grant a term of 75 years renewable for a further 75 years at a rent to be fixed by the director of lands. the land is in the new territories under a block government lease for 75 years from 1 july 1898. a new territories lease was granted for a special purpose and the government has refused to renew it. a non-renewable government lease expired in 1995 and the owner asks what he now holds. a renewable lease expired in 1992 and the owner never executed any renewal paperwork. the lease expires in 2029, carries no right of renewal, and is neither a short term tenancy nor a special purpose lease. the client's lot appears on a non-extension list and he has been given six years' notice. a multi-storey building has 300 owners and the question is how the option to renew could ever be exercised. an easement was granted \"for the residue of the term of the lease\" and the lease has since been renewed by statute."],["PRP-1.07","PCLL8030","LG1","modification","Modifying the lease or the Conditions","modifying the lease or the conditions how a restriction gets changed, which instrument does it, and why the government's refusal is generally unreviewable. a developer wants to build to 40 storeys on a lot restricted to 12 and is willing to pay for the privilege. the conditions restrict user to industrial purposes and the owner wants to convert the building to offices. the government has quoted a premium of several hundred million dollars for the modification and the client says it is irrational. a government lease was modified by a letter rather than by a deed under seal. the director of lands refused an in-situ land exchange and the client wants to apply for judicial review. modification letters were issued for one section of a phased development and the owners of another section say they should have been consulted. the plot ratio and site coverage restrictions have become uncommercial as the district has been rezoned. the client asks which department he should be applying to."],["PRP-1.08","PCLL8030","LG1","user-covenants","Is the use in breach of the user restriction?","is the use in breach of the user restriction? construe the words, measure the offending use, and decide whether what is happening on the land is caught. the lease permits only a private dwelling house and the owner has put up a block of flats. the grant restricts user to residential purposes and the flat is being run as a guesthouse. a covenant prohibits use as a boarding house and the premises have become a home for elderly persons. the conditions permit industrial or godown purposes only and a garment company is testing sample clothing there. a bank is doing data processing in a building restricted to industrial purposes. a unit restricted to industrial use is being used to sell flowers. a logistics business is operating from premises restricted to industrial user, and the landlord knew that was the intention when he let them. one room of a private dwelling is used for charity administration and the rest is lived in. land restricted to agricultural user has subterranean chambers built on it for storing ashes. land permitted to be used for religious purposes now holds 26,000 niches for cremated ashes. the conditions allow each owner one car-parking space and a car-stacking device has been installed. parking is restricted to residents' vehicles and a lorry used commercially is parked there nightly."],["PRP-1.09","PCLL8030","LG1","block-lease-nt","New Territories land: the Watford covenant and Melhado","new territories land: the watford covenant and melhado two clauses that look alike and are not: one in the body of the block government lease binds, the schedule merely describes. the lot is in a demarcation district and the schedule to the block government lease describes it as \"padi\". the government says the owner cannot store shipping containers on land described in the schedule as agricultural. land delineated as agricultural or garden land in a block government lease is being used for building purposes. a structure has been put up on new territories land held under a block government lease and no approval was obtained. the owner argues that the schedule entry \"waste\" fixes the permitted use of his lot for all time. scrapped cars are being stored on new territories agricultural land. the government relies on a covenant in the body of the lease rather than on the schedule. concrete slabs have been laid on agricultural land and are intended to stay in place permanently."],["PRP-1.10","PCLL8030","LG1","offensive-trades","The offensive trades clause","the offensive trades clause a victorian list of trades still sitting in urban government leases — and a general words limb that has outlived the list. a pizza restaurant is trading from premises whose government lease forbids a victualler or tavern-keeper. the premises are let to a sandwich chain and the lease prohibits use by a victualler. a shop sells roast meat and the purchaser's solicitor says the title is defeasible. a home for the elderly is operating from premises whose lease prohibits use as a tavern. a columbarium has been established in a deserted village on land held under a block government lease. the lease forbids the trade of a blacksmith, nightman or scavenger and the client cannot tell whether any of it still matters. the use complained of causes no smell, no noise and nothing visible, and the government says it is still offensive. no licence in writing was ever obtained from the governor or any person authorised in that behalf."],["PRP-1.11","PCLL8030","LG1","ddh-and-approvals","DDH clauses, alienation and other required approvals","ddh clauses, alienation and other required approvals restrictions that work by requiring someone's consent rather than by forbidding a use — and why refusing that consent is usually unchallengeable. the conditions require the director's prior written approval of the design, disposition and height before any building works start. a developer wants to build the tallest building in the world and the director has refused approval on aviation grounds. the government has modified the height and plot ratio restrictions and the developer assumes the ddh clause went with them. the conditions prohibit assignment before the positive conditions are complied with, and the developer wants to pre-sell flats. special condition 7 forbids the purchaser assigning or underletting without the prior written consent of the land officer. a layout plan had to be approved and the government refused it. the director of environmental protection required a noise barrier and the developer double-glazed the windows instead. a developer wants to judicially review a refusal of approval under a clause in the conditions."],["PRP-1.12","PCLL8030","LG1","covenants-running-and-spent","Does the covenant run, and is it spent?","does the covenant run, and is it spent? two separate questions that have to be answered in order before a covenant can be enforced against anybody but the original grantee. the original developer complied with the building covenant in 1965 and the present owner says nothing is left of it. a covenant said the lessee would not erect any building except domestic houses, and a domestic house was duly built. the pavilion required by the conditions was built in 1937, demolished in 2000, and a new complex put up in its place. the government is enforcing a covenant against a purchaser who bought from the developer twenty years ago. the covenant is in a government lease and the client asks whether the cpo provisions apply to it at all. the grantee argues the covenant was exhausted by initial compliance. the lease was renewed by statute and the covenant is said not to have survived the renewal. the director refused consent to a replacement building and the owner says consent was only ever needed for the first one."],["PRP-1.13","PCLL8030","LG1","waiver","Has the Government waived the breach?","has the government waived the breach? three kinds of express waiver, plus implied waiver by acquiescence — and the objective test that tells them apart. a covenant restricted building to one detached residence, a block of flats went up, and the government wrote that it was \"current policy\" not to enforce. a height restriction was waived by correspondence so that one particular block of flats could be built, and that block has since been demolished. the district land office wrote that under current policy a block of flats could be erected, and laco later confirmed after construction that the covenant would not be enforced. a waiver was granted subject to conditions and one of those conditions has since been breached. terraced houses were built before 1939 on land restricted to villa residences, a six-storey block followed in 1958, and neighbouring lots under similar covenants now carry high-rise buildings. the ground floor of a five-storey building has been used as a shop for over fifty years and the government has never objected. the government has taken no enforcement action for decades and the owner says the covenant has gone. the government did nothing about a past breach for fifty years and is now objecting to a proposed new development. the government is actively enforcing and the owner raises waiver as a defence."],["PRP-1.14","PCLL8030","LG1","good-title-no-real-risk","Does an unwaived breach defeat title?","does an unwaived breach defeat title? the conveyancing payoff: between vendor and purchaser the question is not whether there was a breach but whether there is any real risk of enforcement. a crown lease restricted the height to 35 feet, the building exceeds it, and the government has done nothing for forty years. the purchaser raises a requisition asking whether the vendor has shown good title given a longstanding breach. the vendor says the government would never enforce and the purchaser wants to rescind. the government is already taking enforcement action and the owner argues waiver. the premises were granted under conditions restricting use to industrial purposes and have been a flower shop for some years. there is no evidence that the government has been aware of the breach for any significant period. the breach is open, the neighbours have committed the same breach, and completion is next week. the purchaser's solicitor must decide whether to advise completion notwithstanding an unwaived breach."],["PRP-1.15","PCLL8030","LG1","government-implied-covenants","The Government's own covenants","the government's own covenants the grant runs both ways: quiet enjoyment and non-derogation from grant are implied in every government lease and set of conditions. government works have demolished the wall of the tenant's bedroom. the electricity to the premises has been cut off by the grantor. renovations to the building have blocked access to the client's restaurant and profits have fallen. an easement over government land included vehicular use and government works have made the road impassable to vehicles. a latrine has been erected on the land next door to the client's lot. the conditions required part of the land to be used as a public car park, and rezoning has since made the car park commercially worthless. the grantee cannot get plant onto the site because no reasonable access has been provided. the interference complained of lasted a few weeks and has since stopped."],["PRP-2.01","PCLL8030","LG2","developer-sequence","The stages from lot to registered deed of mutual covenant","the stages from lot to registered deed of mutual covenant eight steps turn one lot into many separately owned flats. knowing which step is missing is usually the answer. a developer bought the lot under conditions of sale, built a block of twenty flats and twenty car parking spaces, and now wants to sell them one by one. flats are being sold before the occupation permit has issued and the purchaser asks what authority the developer needed. the assignment to the first purchaser transfers 31 undivided shares of and in the lot together with the exclusive right to occupy flat 5b and car space 12. the deed of mutual covenant was executed by the developer, the first purchaser and a management company that is a subsidiary of the developer. a later assignment from the developer says the flat is sold \"together with the benefit of and subject to the burdens and obligations set out in the deed of mutual covenant\". the deed of mutual covenant was executed but never lodged at the land registry, and the second purchaser now wants to sue the fifth purchaser on a covenant in it. the lot was divided into 620 shares, 30 to each flat and one to each car space, and the penthouse purchaser complains that she has no more shares than anyone else. the developer allocated no undivided shares at all to the staircases, lifts and gardens. the first purchaser wants to know why the assignment gives her shares in the lot rather than ownership of the flat itself."],["PRP-2.02","PCLL8030","LG2","construing-the-dmc","Construing the DMC and the first assignment","construing the dmc and the first assignment what did the developer keep? a question of construction in which the dmc now has the primacy — and the shares tell you more than the words. the developer sold the lower commercial floors and the purchaser now says the roofs and utility rooms, to which no shares were allocated, must be common parts. the dmc defines the common parts by listing them and simply does not mention the external walls at all. the first assignment reserved to the vendor the exclusive use, occupation and enjoyment of the outer walls of the building unless otherwise specifically included in the property. the first assignment reserved the exclusive right to affix and maintain chimneys or flue pipes on the external walls, and to use them for advertising. the dmc allows other owners to use the external wall with the manager's consent and makes the manager responsible for its maintenance and repair. no undivided shares were ever attached to the part of the building now in dispute. the dmc purports to designate the main roof as a common part, but the developer later assigned the exclusive right to part of it to the plaintiff's predecessor. a requisition is raised asking whether roofs and utility rooms retained by the developer are common parts. the developer's retained rights over a part of the building are so extensive that the other owners argue no use is left for them."],["PRP-2.03","PCLL8030","LG2","sub-dmc","Sub-deeds of mutual covenant","sub-deeds of mutual covenant a co-owner may cut up his own shares by deed poll — but new units need new shares, a new plan and, where new common parts appear, a sub-dmc. the owner of the whole 35th floor wants to divide it into eight separate units and sell them. 227 undivided shares were allocated to a floor, the floor was later divided into flats, and no shares were ever allocated to the individual flats. the head dmc is silent on whether an assignee may further divide his shares. a purchaser of one of the new units asks what he is liable to pay by way of management fees under the head dmc. the sole owner of three floors of a commercial building executed a sub-dmc converting them into shops and appointed a manager for those three floors. the incorporated owners of the building are trying to dismiss the manager appointed under a sub-deed for three floors. new corridors and lift lobbies have been created inside a subdivided floor and nobody has said whose they are. only one of several new co-owners under the sub-deed has been sued on a covenant in the head deed. the division of the shares was effected by an assignment rather than by deed poll."],["PRP-2.04","PCLL8030","LG2","undivided-shares-and-good-title","Undivided shares, tenancy in common and good title","undivided shares, tenancy in common and good title what the owner actually owns, what he may sell separately, and when the shares are too vague to give title. the vendor purports to assign the exclusive right to occupy the roof without assigning any undivided shares with it. a mortgage was taken over the flat and the shares, but the entrance hall and staircase were mortgaged separately with no shares attached. the mortgagee has exercised its power of sale over a flat together with an entrance hall to which no shares were ever attached. the sale and purchase agreement sells 12/3,100th shares together with the exclusive right to use unit 912, but the dmc allocated 138 shares to the ninth floor and none to individual units. the land registry control card shows 12 shares against unit 912 and there is a share apportionment document for the development. the vendor agreed to sell a 1/108 share, the dmc allocated no shares, but subdivision registers were opened on the assignments of the flats. a purchaser requisitions the title on the ground that the vendor has not shown how many shares are allocated to the other flats in the building. two co-owners of a flat have died and the survivor claims the whole by survivorship. the exclusive right to occupy a part of the building is being sold on its own, unaccompanied by any share."],["PRP-2.05","PCLL8030","LG2","common-parts-definition","Is it a common part?","is it a common part? section 2 and the first schedule start from the whole building and subtract what has been designated for exclusive use. everything turns on that designation. a co-owner has built a structure on a part of the building and says it was never anybody's common part. the dmc lists the common parts and the part in dispute is not on the list, but nothing says it was retained either. the building was completed in 1965 and the dmc is silent about the disputed part. the dmc assigned 20 undivided shares to the common parts and the definition of common parts includes the external walls. a part of the building has been specified in an instrument registered in the land registry as being for the exclusive use of one owner. the manager is responsible under the dmc for maintaining and repairing the disputed part, and other owners may use it with his consent. the staircase and lift lobby are being used by one owner as storage and the other owners want them back. a pipe running through one owner's flat serves the whole building and the owner objects to the manager entering to repair it. the developer never specified or designated the disputed part in any registered instrument."],["PRP-2.06","PCLL8030","LG2","external-walls","External walls, signs and reserved rights","external walls, signs and reserved rights the commonest fight in the building. a reserved right to advertise is not a reservation of the wall — unless a share went with it. an owner has erected an unauthorised structure on an outside wall and the dmc never delineated the external walls as anyone's. the dmc distinguishes between \"exterior side walls\" and the facade, and the dispute is over the wall at the back of the building. the developer reserved the exclusive right to use the external walls for advertising purposes and to affix chimneys and flue pipes to them. the dmc assigned 20 undivided shares to the common parts and included the external walls in the definition of common parts. the dmc allows other owners to use the external wall with the manager's consent, and makes the manager responsible for its maintenance. an undivided share was assigned specifically to the external wall and reserved by the developer. a co-owner has affixed a large trade name sign advertising a pre-school to the external wall. the developer says its reserved rights over the external wall are so extensive that no use is left for the other owners. the incorporated owners are being sued over a defective air grille in the external wall and say the wall is the developer's."],["PRP-2.07","PCLL8030","LG2","internal-walls","Internal, load-bearing and partition walls","internal, load-bearing and partition walls schedule 1 catches load-bearing walls, but an ordinary partition between two flats belongs to the owners either side. the owner of two adjacent flats has cut a doorway through the wall between them for access. a partition wall divides two flats and neither assignment says the wall is for the exclusive use of either owner. the dmc and the other title deeds are silent about the wall now being demolished. expert evidence is called to say that the wall in question does not in fact bear any load. an owner wants to know whether he may demolish a wall wholly inside his own flat. the incorporated owners say the wall is a common part and the owner replies that a common area inside his own flat is an unattractive idea. the assignment of the flat says nothing about the wall, and the argument is about what the assignor had power to assign. the demolition is said to be both a conversion of a common part and a structural alteration. the wall being altered runs between two flats in the same ownership."],["PRP-2.08","PCLL8030","LG2","bmo-overriding-duties","The BMO's overriding duties: sections 34H and 34I","the bmo's overriding duties: sections 34h and 34i two statutory duties that override anything the dmc says: repair what you exclusively occupy, and keep off the common parts. a co-owner has converted part of the lobby into a shop and installed a large vending machine elsewhere in the common parts. a co-owner has put surveillance cameras up in the common corridor without asking anybody. the dmc expressly permits the very conduct that the ordinance prohibits. the owners' committee passed a resolution approving one owner's conversion of part of the common parts. the owners' committee refused consent to a conversion and the owner complains that the refusal was unreasonable. a meeting of all the owners, rather than the owners' committee, purported to approve the conversion. a stranger to the dmc, who owns nothing in the building, has taken over part of the common parts. the developer reserved a right to erect signs and flue pipes on the external wall and now denies any duty to repair it. storage of personal belongings in the common area next to the car park is said to be unlawful."],["PRP-2.09","PCLL8030","LG2","structural-alterations","Structural alterations and external appearance","structural alterations and external appearance an express covenant in every dmc, construed more liberally than the buildings ordinance — and one set of works often breaches two covenants. an owner has constructed a new window in the outside wall of his flat. an owner demolished part of the outside wall so that the unit could be used as a garage, and produced an architect's certificate saying the structure was unaffected. air conditioners have been installed in the outside wall of a flat. an owner has enlarged the window in the outside wall of his flat. an owner has affixed his trade name to the outside wall of the building. concrete plinths and gondolas have been installed on the roof of the building. a door has been cut through a load-bearing wall between two flats in the same ownership. the works are said to be structural under the dmc although they would not be under the buildings ordinance. the alterations are visible from the street and the dmc forbids altering the external appearance of the building."],["PRP-2.10","PCLL8030","LG2","nuisance-and-annoyance","Nuisance, annoyance, and entry to abate","nuisance, annoyance, and entry to abate annoyance is easier to prove than nuisance, so read the covenant's exact words — and know how to get into the offending flat. a co-owner has erected antennae on the roof and other owners fear the radiation is harming their health. the dmc forbids any act which may be or become \"a nuisance or annoyance or cause damage\" to the other owners. the dmc forbids only the commission of \"a nuisance\" and says nothing about annoyance. water is seeping from one unit in a multi-storey building into the co-owner's unit below. an air-conditioning condenser has been erected which blocks the view to the display window of the shop next door. residents complain of loud and disturbing noise from a neighbouring unit but no expert evidence is available. an owner refuses to let the incorporated owners' workmen into his flat to replace a defective air grille in the external wall. a pipe inside one owner's flat is affecting the common parts and the management committee wants to inspect it. no scientific proof of a health hazard is available and the respondent applies for further and better particulars."],["PRP-2.11","PCLL8030","LG2","adverse-possession","Adverse possession in a multi-storey building","adverse possession in a multi-storey building a co-owner is entitled to use the whole lot, so nothing short of ousting everyone will do — and whether the common parts can be possessed at all is still open. one co-owner has used part of the building exclusively for twenty-five years and now claims a possessory title. a co-owner has collected and kept all the rent from the land since 1978 without accounting to the other co-owners. the claimant expressly disavows any intention to eject the other co-owners from the land. one of the paper title owners occasionally entered the land and parked his car there without paying. the wall of one shop encroaches on part of the neighbouring shop and has done so for the limitation period. the area claimed was separated from the neighbour's shop by a concrete wall and has formed part of the claimant's shop. the defendants asserted no title over the area until after the action was commenced. the incorporated owners have built a management office on part of the roof and claim it by possession. the area said to be adversely possessed is a common part of the building rather than anyone's unit."],["PRP-2.12","PCLL8030","LG2","proper-plaintiff","Who is the proper plaintiff?","who is the proper plaintiff? three kinds of building, three answers. get this wrong and the action is struck out however good the breach. a co-owner has commenced an action over a structural alteration to the common parts and the building is incorporated. a co-owner applies for a declaration that another co-owner's signboard on the exterior wall breaches the dmc. the building has a dmc and an owners' committee but the owners have never incorporated. the building has no dmc at all and one owner wants to stop another's works. water is seeping from the roof, a common part, into one owner's flat and nobody else's. the dmc empowers the manager to enforce its covenants and the manager has declined to take action. the client wants to know whether to complain to the owner, the manager or the incorporated owners. the owners' committee has resolved to sue in its own name. only some of the co-owners are affected by the breach and they have suffered specific damage."],["PRP-2.13","PCLL8030","LG2","io-duties-and-liability","The owners' corporation: duties and liabilities","the owners' corporation: duties and liabilities a legal person with a statutory job description — and, because it controls the common parts, an occupier's exposure to anyone the building hurts. water has seeped from the roof, a common part, into the flat below and the owner sues the incorporated owners and the management company. the incorporated owners were told of a defect months ago and did nothing about it. a concrete canopy over the pavement collapsed, killing a pedestrian and injuring others. a piece of concrete fell from a balcony illegally extended thirty-five years ago and killed a hawker below. most of the co-defendants held liable for the accident are insolvent and the incorporated owners are not. the incorporated owners themselves installed a water pipe in breach of the dmc. the management committee has employed a manager, insured the building and opened a bank account, and an owner questions its authority. an owner argues that s18(1) makes the incorporated owners automatically liable for any disrepair in the common parts. the incorporated owners have engaged independent contractors to carry out the repairs."],["PRP-2.14","PCLL8030","LG2","acquiescence","Acquiescence after Centre Chase","acquiescence after centre chase the court of final appeal has swept away the rule that an incorporated owners cannot acquiesce. what is left is proprietary estoppel — and the question whether it binds successors. an owner demolished the external wall on the basis of an approval, and the incorporated owners took no action for four years afterwards. a huge advertising sign was erected on an external canopy and the dmc gave the developer liberty to permit such signs. an owner affixed his trade name to a common part and the incorporated owners knew about it and did nothing. fung shui window frames were affixed on top of the existing window frames on an external wall. the breach complained of is an unreasonable interference with the common parts rather than a conversion of them. the incorporated owners say they were duty bound to enforce and so could not have acquiesced. the owner who is said to have acquiesced has since sold the flat, and the new owner now sues. the breach was committed before the owners incorporated and the incorporated owners now object to it. an owner spent a large sum on works after the manager indicated that no objection would be taken."],["PRP-2.15","PCLL8030","LG2","remedies-and-charge","Remedies: injunction, damages and the charge","remedies: injunction, damages and the charge a negative covenant deliberately broken gets an injunction as of course; and unpaid management fees get a charge, but only if the dmc provides for one. the owner erected a structure in breach of a negative covenant with his eyes open and now argues the balance of convenience. the developer converted part of the common parts to its own use and the question is how damages are to be assessed. an owner has occupied part of the common parts for six years and the incorporated owners want to be paid for it. noise levels from a unit are in breach of the dmc and the plaintiff wants the activity stopped. management fees have gone unpaid for two years and the manager wants to impose a charge on the flat. the incorporated owners purported to impose a charge and the manager purported to impose one personally as well. the dmc contains no provision at all empowering the imposition of a charge for unpaid management fees. a charge was imposed but never registered, and the flat has been sold to a bona fide purchaser. a mandatory injunction is sought requiring the removal of structures already built."],["PRP-2.16","PCLL8030","LG2","enforceability-s41","Does the covenant run? Section 41 of the Conveyancing and Property Ordinance","does the covenant run? section 41 of the conveyancing and property ordinance benefit and burden run by different routes. test them separately, and remember positive covenants do not bind tenants or occupiers. the plaintiff is the fifth purchaser of a flat and was never a party to the dmc, which was executed in 1978. the defendant is a tenant of a co-owner and the covenant sued on requires money to be spent on repairs. the covenant relied on gives one owner the right to name the building. the covenant is to repay a tenant's deposit and the landlord has assigned the reversion. the dmc says the covenants are made \"for the benefit of the land of the covenantee and his successors in title\". the dmc is silent on whether the covenants are for the benefit of successors in title. there is no dmc at all, but the development was laid out and sold as a single scheme. one house on a small-house development never executed the dmc and a purchaser raises a requisition about it. the covenantee has sold every unit he owned and now wants to enforce the covenant. a squatter in part of the building is sued on a covenant in the dmc."],["PRP-2.17","PCLL8030","LG2","predecessor-breaches","Liability for a predecessor in title's breach","liability for a predecessor in title's breach a positive covenant breach dies with the seller; a restrictive one may not, if the breach continues and the buyer has adopted it. the previous owner erected an iron gate blocking the common parts and the present owner has left it there. structural alterations were made to the external wall of the house by the previous owner and the incorporated owners now want it reinstated. part of the common parts was converted by a predecessor in title and the present owner continues to use it. the vendor left management fees unpaid for two years and the purchaser is now asked to pay them. the dmc provides that the \"owner for the time being\" is liable for past and present management charges. the defendant received the property from his father as a gift rather than by purchase. a party to the dmc has sold his interest and is being sued for something he did before he sold. a purchaser raises a requisition on unauthorised works erected by the vendor's predecessor. the works were done before the present owner bought, and he has done nothing to remove them."],["PRP-3.01","PCLL8030","LG3","conveyancing-stages","The stages of a simple conveyancing transaction","the stages of a simple conveyancing transaction twenty-odd steps from the estate agent's office to the sending of the accounts. most title questions are really \"which stage is this?\". a preliminary agreement was signed in the estate agent's office and a preliminary deposit paid. the purchaser wants to know whether the preliminary agreement he signed a fortnight ago needs stamping. one firm of solicitors proposes to act for both the vendor and the purchaser. title deeds have been sent to the purchaser's solicitors against an undertaking to return them on demand. on execution of the formal agreement a further tranche of deposit brings the total paid to 10 per cent of the price. the assignment has been executed in escrow by the purchaser and sent to the vendor. completion is to take place by mutual undertakings between the two firms of solicitors. the purchase price less the deposits was sent by cashier order against an undertaking to send the executed assignment and title deeds within 21 days of completion. the property being sold is already subject to a mortgage the vendor has not yet discharged."],["PRP-3.02","PCLL8030","LG3","twin-duties","The twin duties of giving and showing title","the twin duties of giving and showing title two separate and independent duties. breach of either lets the purchaser rescind, and a good title is no answer to a bad requisition answer. the vendor in fact has a perfect title but has answered the purchaser's requisition with a bare assertion. the purchaser never raised a requisition at all, and at completion the vendor cannot give good title. a requisition was raised out of time and the vendor was entitled to refuse to answer it. the purchaser refuses to complete on the ground that the vendor has not produced a document the agreement required. the vendor says he has answered every requisition and so must be taken to have given good title. the agreement requires the vendor to give title in accordance with s13a and to prove title in accordance with s13. the purchaser purports to rescind at completion over a defect he knew about all along. the vendor has removed the defect but has produced no evidence that it has gone. the dispute is whether the breach is repudiatory or sounds only in damages."],["PRP-3.03","PCLL8030","LG3","what-is-good-title","What constitutes a good title?","what constitutes a good title? not a perfect title. the test is whether there is a real risk the title is encumbered or defeasible, or whether the risk is merely illusory. a purchaser refuses to complete over a possibility of an encumbrance that nobody has ever asserted. a title document dating from the 1920s is missing and the government has no record of it either. the vendor says the risk of the alleged encumbrance being asserted is fanciful. an unauthorised cockloft has stood in a shop in an older district for decades without any enforcement action. the purchaser demands that every conceivable blemish be cleared before he will complete. a deed of mutual covenant executed twenty years ago may have been invalidly executed, and nobody has ever complained. the court is asked to declare in favour of good title on a vendor and purchaser summons. the alleged defect is one of defeasibility — a right of re-entry — rather than an encumbrance. both sides are said to have intended to see the transaction through to completion on the terms of their bargain."],["PRP-3.04","PCLL8030","LG3","duty-to-give-title","The duty to give good title","the duty to give good title no rule of law compels it — the agreement does. and where it does, ask whether the legal estate or only the equitable interest must pass. the agreement adopts condition 9 of part a of the second schedule to the conveyancing and property ordinance. the sale and purchase agreement says nothing at all about title. the vendor argues that he never promised good title, only such title as he happens to have. the vendor can assign only an equitable interest because no certificate of compliance has yet issued. an uncompleted unit in a development is being sold under the consent scheme after consent to assign but before the certificate of compliance. the purchaser insists on being assigned the legal estate and the vendor says the equitable interest is enough. the agreement is an open contract with no express provision about the vendor's obligations. a pre-sale under the consent scheme is in issue and the developer has not yet had the legal estate vested in it. the parties dispute what was in their contemplation about the legal estate when the government gave consent to assign."],["PRP-3.05","PCLL8030","LG3","exclusion-clauses","Excluding or limiting the duty to give title","excluding or limiting the duty to give title contra proferentem, and a clause that would let the vendor mislead the purchaser will not be construed to do it. the agreement says the purchaser will accept the title of the vendor and agrees to raise no requisitions thereon. the agreement says the vendor agrees to sell only such title as he holds. the purchaser is deemed to purchase with full knowledge of all defects and to assume that a named power of attorney was valid and unrevoked. the clause excludes defects in the condition of the property and the defect complained of is one of title. the vendor's solicitors knew of the defect and the purchaser did not. the purchaser was acting for a professional speculator and bought several houses in the same development. the vendor gave no warranty as to unauthorised structures and the purchaser now complains of illegal structures. an occupation permit restricted the premises to office use and they were plainly being run as a karaoke bar. the formal agreement contains a limiting clause that the binding preliminary agreement did not. the agreement contains an \"as is\" clause and the complaint is about unauthorised building works."],["PRP-3.06","PCLL8030","LG3","purchaser-waiver","Waiver by the purchaser of his right to object","waiver by the purchaser of his right to object three requirements: knowledge of the defect, knowledge of its legal significance, and an unequivocal act. not raising a requisition is none of them. the purchaser, knowing of the defect, sent the draft assignment to the vendor for execution. clear words in correspondence between the parties show the purchaser accepting the title notwithstanding the defect. the purchaser had been informed of the alterations and the contractual time limit for raising requisitions had passed. the purchaser wrote letters that did not raise the cockloft point but reserved the right to raise further requisitions. the purchaser inspected the flat before entering into the agreement and only later raised a requisition about unauthorised partitioning. an experienced buyer signed the provisional agreement knowing the cockloft was an irremovable unauthorised structure. the purchaser never raised any requisition and the vendor says he has thereby accepted the title. the purchaser knew the facts but says he did not understand their legal consequences. the vendor argues that the purchaser elected to affirm the contract with full knowledge of his right to rescind."],["PRP-3.07","PCLL8030","LG3","encumbered-or-defeasible","Encumbered and defeasible titles","encumbered and defeasible titles an encumbrance is a third-party interest in the land; defeasibility is a real risk the title will be taken away. different labels, different consequences. a third party claims an equitable interest in the flat under a resulting trust. an uncompleted sale and purchase agreement is registered against the title. the original title deeds were deposited with a lender and nothing was registered. the government has served a notice of re-entry for breach of a covenant in the conditions of grant. the building authority has remedied unauthorised building works and may sell the property to recover its expenses. the incorporated owners are threatening enforcement action over a significant breach of the deed of mutual covenant. a mortgagee is exercising its power of sale over the property being sold. the assignment to the present owner is liable to be set aside as a conveyance to defraud creditors. a right of way and a restrictive covenant are both registered against the property."],["PRP-3.08","PCLL8030","LG3","title-in-vendor","Title in the vendor, consents, and assigning the whole interest","title in the vendor, consents, and assigning the whole interest three checks before anything else: does he own it, may he sell it without asking anyone, and can he pass it free of third-party interests? the land the vendor has agreed to assign is still in the ownership of a t'so. no consent of the land officer has been obtained under s15 of the new territories ordinance. the property is owned by a company rather than by the vendor, but the vendor controls the company absolutely. the consent of a t'so is required before the manager may sell. the property is being sold under the consent scheme and the director of lands has not yet consented. the vendor has agreed to sell land he does not own but says he can compel the owner to assign. the purchaser asks whether the vendor can assign free from all encumbrances. a third party's consent is needed before the assignment can be executed at all. the sale includes adjoining land held in a different ownership from the flat itself."],["PRP-3.09","PCLL8030","LG3","unregistered-encumbrances","Registrable encumbrances that are unregistered","registrable encumbrances that are unregistered void against a purchaser for value under s3(2) of the land registration ordinance — and his knowledge of them is irrelevant. a five-year lease was granted and never registered, and the reversion has now been sold. a declaration of trust in writing was never registered against the property. an option to renew in a tenancy agreement was not registered and the reversion has been assigned. the assignment of the reversion was expressly stated to be subject to tenancies the landlord had failed to register. a right to exclusive use and occupation of the roof was omitted from the memorial on registration. the purchaser knew about the unregistered tenancy before he bought and the tenant says that defeats him. a written tenancy for two years was granted at a rent 15 per cent below market. a tenancy for three years or less was created orally and the landlord never registered anything. the purchaser and vendor are alleged to have connived to evict a tenant whose lease was unregistered."],["PRP-3.10","PCLL8030","LG3","patent-defects","Encumbrances expressly subject to, and patent defects","encumbrances expressly subject to, and patent defects the other two exceptions: what the contract names, and what anyone could see by looking. the schedule to the formal agreement identifies the deed of mutual covenant, a right of way and a restrictive covenant. every sale of a unit in the multi-storey building is made expressly subject to the covenants in the deed of mutual covenant. a light well has been converted into actual structure, visible to anyone who looks at the building. a right of passage over a staircase and hallway inside a multi-storey building is in dispute. the vendor did not tell the purchaser about a defect he says was obvious. the premises were plainly being run as a karaoke bar although the occupation permit allowed only office use. the purchaser says he could not have been expected to notice the encroachment. the encumbrance was disclosed in the agreement and the purchaser now objects to it. the vendor relies on the defect being \"patent to the eye\"."],["PRP-3.11","PCLL8030","LG3","original-title-deeds","Original title deeds: common law and section 13A","original title deeds: common law and section 13a delivery of originals is part of giving title, and it exists to keep an encumbrance off the purchaser. de monsa cuts the duty by that purpose; s13a cuts it by s13's 15-year period. the agreement is dated 3 march 2015 and the vendor can produce certified copies, but not the originals, of three assignments in the chain of title. the sale and purchase agreement was signed on 2 may 2008 and the vendor says s13a governs what he must hand over. the agreement provides that the vendor shall give title in accordance with the common law rather than s13a. the vendor's solicitor has sworn a statutory declaration saying that after extensive searches he cannot explain how the original deeds came to be missing. the purchaser objects that the missing originals may have been deposited to secure an equitable mortgage. two sale and purchase agreements registered against the property were never completed, and both purchasers were finance companies. the flat is a home ownership scheme flat subject to the alienation restrictions in s17b of the housing ordinance. only the originals of documents more than 15 years old are missing, and the vendor says he need not account for them. the intermediate root refers to a deed of easement, and the purchaser demands delivery of the original of that deed as well. the vendor offers a certified copy taken from a photocopy, and the purchaser objects to it."],["PRP-3.12","PCLL8030","LG3","documents-of-title","Which documents are documents of title?","which documents are documents of title? a working list, and the question that runs through it: does this document relate exclusively to the property, and could it affect the purchaser? the vendor refuses to produce the occupation permit for a building constructed in 1950. the deed of mutual covenant for a small house development deals only with common areas and allocates no shares. a sub-deed of mutual covenant exists for the floor on which the unit sits. the certificate of compliance relates to conditions dated before 1 january 1970. the land was sectioned by deed poll and the purchaser asks for it. the vendor offers a grant of probate and the purchaser says he wants letters of administration. a car park layout plan has been superseded by an amended plan. an instrument is registered only in the deeds pending registration section of the land register. a permit was issued by government allowing a balcony to overhang government land. the occupation permit does not mention the car parking space being sold."],["PRP-4.01","PCLL8030","LG4","showing-vs-giving","Showing title: production and requisitions","showing title: production and requisitions two limbs: produce the documents, and answer the requisitions. a perfect title is no defence to failing either. the vendor has produced every document the agreement required but has answered a requisition with a bare assertion. the purchaser rescinded at completion although the vendor could in fact have proved a perfect title. a requisition was raised out of time and the vendor refused to answer it; at completion the title was still not good. the vendor says he has discharged his obligations because he complied with s13 of the conveyancing and property ordinance. the purchaser wants to know whether he must accept certified copies rather than originals. the agreement requires proof of title in accordance with s13 and the giving of title in accordance with s13a. the purchaser says the vendor must both produce the deeds and explain the gap in the chain. the vendor asks whether answering a requisition cures a defect in the title itself. a document produced by way of showing title turns out to reveal a defect in the title."],["PRP-4.02","PCLL8030","LG4","statutory-period-s13","The statutory period for showing title","the statutory period for showing title fifteen years, or since the grant if the grant is younger. section 13(1) is the default; the agreement may lengthen or shorten it. the government lease was granted in 2018 and the sale and purchase agreement is dated 2026. the conditions of grant date from 1962 and the vendor proposes to start the title at an assignment of 2007. the agreement says the vendor shall prove title in accordance with s13 \"commencing with the government lease of the said premises\". the purchaser insists on being shown title back beyond the intermediate root. a document is referred to in the intermediate root assignment and has not been produced. a power of attorney was used to execute an assignment eleven years ago. the vendor refuses to produce any document dating from before the intermediate root. the vendor offers a certified copy of a certified copy of an assignment. the vendor offers a certified copy of a plain photocopy of an assignment."],["PRP-4.03","PCLL8030","LG4","ultimate-root","The ultimate root of title","the ultimate root of title the government lease or conditions — plus anything the interpretation ordinance folds into that definition, including modifications, surrenders and marginal notes. a letter of modification varying the conditions of grant has not been produced. a deed of surrender was executed in 1978 and the vendor says it is outside the title period. two letters from the director of public works dated 1923 to 1930 which contained terms of the conditions of grant are missing. the lands department has no record of the missing letters either. the certified copy of the crown lease produced by the vendor omits a marginal note. a marginal note refers to a licence granted after the lease which varied its terms. the government lease is illegible and the vendor has produced the illegible copy. the agreement expressly required the vendor to provide a legible copy of the conditions. the government lease itself cannot be found at all."],["PRP-4.04","PCLL8030","LG4","intermediate-root","The intermediate root of title","the intermediate root of title an assignment, a mortgage by assignment or a legal charge, each dealing with the whole estate and interest. nothing less will do. the vendor proposes to start the title at a discharge of mortgage dated just over 15 years before the contract. the only document more than 15 years old dealing with the property is an equitable mortgage. the vendor holds only an equitable interest because the positive conditions have never been complied with. there is no document in existence that could serve as an intermediate root. the intermediate root assignment refers to a deed of assignment registered by a memorial number and that deed is missing. all the chain of title assignments say the shop is sold subject to exceptions and reservations set out in an earlier assignment. the intermediate root mentions the first assignment, the deed of mutual covenant and the certificate of compliance. the government lease is only eight years old and the purchaser asks for an intermediate root anyway. the vendor refuses to explain why s13(1)(b) does not apply to a document referred to in the root."],["PRP-4.05","PCLL8030","LG4","chain-of-title","The chain of title and registration of title documents","the chain of title and registration of title documents produce every document in the period, prove every link — and make sure the ones that pass title have actually been registered. the assignment to the present vendor remains in the land registry as a deed pending registration. a discharge of charge in the chain of title was never registered. a sale and purchase agreement in the chain was never registered but a properly registered assignment followed it. an unregistered mortgage appears among the documents supplied. the vendor has produced the documents but has not explained how the property passed from one owner to the next. a 1979 pump pit agreement has been replaced by a 1988 agreement, and the purchaser demands both. a car park layout plan has been amended and the purchaser wants the superseded version. the certificate of compliance is unregistered and the purchaser questions the conversion of the equitable interest. the deed of mutual covenant is unregistered and the purchaser questions the enforceability of its covenants."],["PRP-4.06","PCLL8030","LG4","pre-intermediate-defects","Pre-intermediate root title defects","pre-intermediate root title defects the vendor need not produce documents before the root — but if the purchaser finds a defect there, the vendor must still answer for it. the purchaser obtained pre-intermediate root documents from the land registry himself. during the pre-intermediate root period an attorney wrongfully made a gift of the property. a deed in the pre-intermediate root period was executed by only one director of a company. the vendor says he need not answer because the defect falls outside the statutory period. the vendor argues that the burden is on the purchaser to prove the pre-intermediate root defect first. the purchaser raises a requisition on a document the vendor was never obliged to supply. a severance of a joint tenancy in the pre-intermediate root period cannot be proved. the occupation permit from the pre-intermediate root period is missing. a recital in a later assignment refers to the pre-intermediate root document in issue."],["PRP-4.07","PCLL8030","LG4","powers-of-attorney","Powers of attorney under section 13(1)(c)","powers of attorney under section 13(1)(c) produce any power under which a produced document was executed in the last 15 years — the vendor's side, and the present assignment too. an assignment in the chain of title was executed under a power of attorney eleven years ago. the present assignment is to be executed by the vendor's attorney. the purchaser demands the power of attorney under which he himself is executing. conditions of grant were executed by way of a power of attorney on behalf of a purchaser. a power of attorney used sixteen years ago is the only one in the chain. the purchaser is asked to assume, by a limiting clause, that a named power of attorney was valid and unrevoked. the vendor cannot locate the power under which an assignment in the chain was signed. a power of attorney relates exclusively to the property and the question is whether the original must be handed over. the attorney who executed a pre-intermediate root document is said to have exceeded his authority."],["PRP-4.08","PCLL8030","LG4","missing-deeds-re-halifax","Missing title deeds and the rule in Re Halifax","missing title deeds and the rule in re halifax three things: a statutory declaration explaining the loss, secondary evidence of the contents, and proof of due execution. neither the original nor a certified copy of an assignment in the chain can be found. the vendor offers the old form memorial as a substitute for the missing title document. the government lease itself has been lost and the vendor proposes secondary evidence. a statutory declaration has been made by someone who has no personal knowledge of how the documents were lost. original title deeds were lost in the japanese invasion or destroyed by damp and storage. the vendor produces a statutory declaration but no evidence of due execution. the purchaser accepts the contents of the missing deed but disputes that it was properly executed. the vendor says de monsa means he need not account for the missing originals at all. the absence of the original suggests a deposit of title deeds to secure a loan."],["PRP-4.09","PCLL8030","LG4","recitals","Using recitals in proving title","using recitals in proving title two provisions, two different jobs: s13(3) presumes a recited pre-root document; s13(4) proves a fact recited in one. an assignment in the chain recites a crown lease that cannot be found. a recital in a subsequent assignment refers to a distribution agreement which is missing. a deed of mutual covenant of 1931 and an assignment of 1932 recite that the terms of the grant were duly accepted and deposited in the land office. two letters referred to in a pre-intermediate root document cannot be located. a severance of a joint tenancy in the pre-intermediate root period cannot be directly proved. the occupation permit from the pre-intermediate root period is missing but is recited in a later deed. the purchaser says a recital proves nothing because the underlying document has never been seen. the vendor relies on a recital and the purchaser offers no evidence to the contrary. the document containing the recital is itself more than 15 years old."],["PRP-4.10","PCLL8030","LG4","mere-conveyance","Matters of mere conveyance","matters of mere conveyance a defect the vendor can remove on his own, without anyone else's concurrence, does not make the title defective. the property is still subject to a mortgage which the vendor intends to discharge before completion. trespassers are occupying the land the vendor has agreed to sell with vacant possession. licensees are on the land and the vendor says he can remove them. trespassers occupy wall stalls outside a shop in a multi-storey building. the occupants of the property are tenants under a registered lease rather than trespassers. the purchaser refuses to complete because of a subsisting charge the vendor has undertaken to discharge. the vendor says the defect will be gone by completion and the purchaser says that is too late. the vendor needs a third party's cooperation to remove the defect. an unauthorised structure could be demolished before completion."],["PRP-4.11","PCLL8030","LG4","investigating-title","Investigating title: what the purchaser's solicitor must do","investigating title: what the purchaser's solicitor must do two searches, documents back to the ultimate root, a site visit, the plans — then reasonable requisitions. failing to search may be negligence. a lis pendens was registered against the property and the purchaser's solicitor never searched. only one land registry search was carried out, three months before completion. the solicitor relied on the documents the vendor supplied and obtained nothing from the land registry himself. an encumbrance was registered in the week before completion. the plans annexed to the government lease are not colour coded. nobody has visited the property to see who is in occupation. a purchaser is suing his own solicitor for failing to discover a defect before completion. the solicitor did not check whether there was a right of way giving access to the property. no structural survey was arranged before the agreement was signed."],["PRP-4.12","PCLL8030","LG4","raising-requisitions","Raising requisitions: the time limits","raising requisitions: the time limits no duty to raise one — but if you do, do it within the express time, or within a reasonable time if the agreement is silent. the agreement adopts condition 7 of part a of the second schedule to the conveyancing and property ordinance. the agreement requires requisitions to be raised within 7 days after receipt of the title deeds. title deeds arrived in three batches and the purchaser waited for the last before raising anything. the agreement allowed 9 days from agreement to completion and the title deeds arrived 7 days before completion. the agreement is silent as to when requisitions must be raised. the vendor supplied the title deeds two months before completion and the requisitions were raised 9 days before it. a requisition was properly raised two days before the completion date. the purchaser suspects a defect and is minded to say nothing until completion. the purchaser was six minutes late completing because the vendor gave directions for split cheques at 11:13 am."],["PRP-4.13","PCLL8030","LG4","requisitions-out-of-time","Raising requisitions out of time","raising requisitions out of time the exception is a requisition going to the root of the vendor's title — and the exception to that is one the purchaser should have raised with due diligence. the government lease is missing and the purchaser raises the point after the contractual deadline. a cockloft in a shop in an older district is the subject of a requisition raised late. an illegal staircase has been demolished and a cockloft converted into an additional storey. there is significant encroachment over a public street. the purchaser asks late whether there is a right of way giving him ingress and egress. a co-owner has sued over demolition of part of an external wall to create an additional building entrance used for 30 years. an unauthorised swimming pool has been built and there is no real risk of re-entry by government. the purchaser repeats an earlier requisition in different words after the deadline. the defect could have been spotted from the title deeds supplied weeks earlier. the vendor answers a late requisition without saying anything about his right to refuse."],["PRP-4.14","PCLL8030","LG4","subject-matter-of-requisitions","The subject matter of requisitions","the subject matter of requisitions matters of title, specific and precisely formulated. not speculative, not fishing, and not about the state of the building. the purchaser asks whether the vendor knows of anything which might adversely affect his title. the purchaser asks whether there have been any breaches of the height restriction applicable to the building. the purchaser asks the vendor to advise whether there are any unauthorised or illegal structures or alterations in the property. the requisition complains of a leaking roof and defective electrics. the requisition is framed in general terms without identifying any specific defect. the requisition identifies the defect but does not suggest how it should be remedied. the vendor refuses to answer on the ground that the requisition is frivolous. the purchaser raises a long list of questions hoping one will produce something. the requisition relates to dry rot rather than to anything on the title."],["PRP-4.15","PCLL8030","LG4","answering-requisitions","Answering requisitions with candour","answering requisitions with candour adequately, with candour, in time, and with the facts set out. a bald assertion is a failure to show title even if the title is good. the occupation permit shows seven flats and there are nine on the floor. the vendor began responding four days before completion and sent an authorised person's certificate two days before it. the vendor says a possible invalidity in the execution of a deed of mutual covenant is not a defect because the deed is over 20 years old. the vendor replies that he has removed the unauthorised structure but produces no evidence of removal. the vendor did not check the conditions of grant before answering a requisition about the number of storeys. the vendor suggests that the point be resolved by a vendor and purchaser summons. the vendor undertakes to remedy the defect after completion. a certificate the agreement required was provided at 6.07 pm on the completion date. the purchaser wrote \"noted\" in response to the vendor's answer. the purchaser extended time for answering by enclosing the draft assignment subject to compliance by completion."]];
